 Peer Review","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.25701"},{"title":"Why are the \"rating\" and \"confidence\" fields in my PC Console wrong? | OpenReview","url":"https://docs.openreview.net/getting-started/frequently-asked-questions/why-are-the-rating-and-confidence-fields-in-my-pc-console-wrong"}]},"Based on my search results, I found information about OpenReview's default review form structure, though the specific CoRL review form details weren't fully captured. Here's what I found:\n\nOpenReview's default review form includes a rating scale with values from 10 down to 4, where 10 represents \"Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", 9 represents \"Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", 8 represents \"Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", 7 represents \"Good paper, accept\", 6 represents \"Marginally above acceptance threshold\", 5 represents \"Marginally below acceptance threshold\", and 4 represents \"Ok but not good enough\".\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain the complete specific information about CoRL's exact OpenReview review form with the precise \"1:\" to \"10:\" overall score scale and recommendation categories you're looking for. To get the exact details of CoRL's review form, you may need to:\n\n1. Check the actual CoRL OpenReview page directly during an active submission period\n2. Look at the CoRL reviewer guidelines page for the specific year you're interested in\n3. Contact the CoRL program chairs directly\n\nWould you like me to search for information about a specific year of CoRL (such as CoRL 2024 or 2025)?"],"durationSeconds":9.888885836000002}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VvfcTWFfCNeSNGEzVqowPu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I now have enough context to establish the CoRL review scoring scale. Based on the OpenReview default review form (which CoRL uses on OpenReview), the scale is:\n\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough - Loss of significant contribution\n\n\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nWith CoRL's typical 30-35% acceptance rate in mind, I need to dispatch two reviewers in parallel—one focused on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work—then conduct the full roundtable discussion. Let me organize the paper content and review context I've gathered so far.\n\nThe paper is a 10-page analysis examining how fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing in vision-language-action models across three architectures. The key findings are that VLA training reorganizes instruction attention from a mid-layer inverted-U pattern to an entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern, and that R² exit collapse happens specifically at the VLM-to-VLA boundary rather than within VLA-to-VLA transitions. The analysis spans OpenVLA, TracVLA, and NORA models tested on LIBERO task suites, with supporting evidence from routing gradients and vision encoder geometry.\n\nThe CF sensitivity analysis is being downgraded to supporting evidence due to metric incomparability issues—comparing trained Cohen's d against untrained cosine distance—and CKA reversals that undermine the RSA claims. I'm projecting reviewer scores around 6.0 with roughly 35% acceptance probability and 50% rebuttal success. For CoRL specifically, I need to ensure the robotics relevance is clear since it's a robotics and learning venue with 30-35% acceptance, uses OpenReview's 1-10 scale, and requires a limitations section. I'm now setting up parallel reviewer simulations to stress-test the paper's arguments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1634,"cache_read_input_tokens":63649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1634},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"2be9c748-83d9-43da-a182-143a03d6a0c7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VvfcTWFfCNeSNGEzVqowPu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all inputs. Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1634,"cache_read_input_tokens":63649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1634},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"92daaf13-a670-421b-b50c-43f948b09ea5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VvfcTWFfCNeSNGEzVqowPu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (OpenReview, ~32% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nConfidence: 1 (low) to 5 (high)\n\nCoRL requires relevance to robot learning. Papers must include a limitations section; treat honestly reported limitations kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\nThis is an analysis/interpretability paper (not a new model or new method for robot control). It studies how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing at the token level in decoder-only VLA models. It examines 3 architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. **Routing reorganization (C1):** VLA training shifts instruction attention from a mid-layer inverted-U pattern to an entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern. Trained models achieve 99.6% L1 instruction probe accuracy vs 80.2% for pretrained base. 24/32 attention heads become instruction-dominant at L1 (vs 9/32 pretrained).\n2. **R² exit collapse (C2):** Action-relevant information peaks mid-layer and is discarded at exit layers, but ONLY during VLM→VLA transition (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455), NOT during VLA→VLA fine-tuning (0/4 cells, Δ=0.033–0.086). A one-time reorganization.\n3. **Three-tier routing gradient (C3):** VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA, progressive routing development.\n4. **Vision encoder geometry (observation, qualified):** Non-monotonic RSA profile, but CKA yields different ordering. Explicitly marked as metric-dependent observation, not a robust finding.\n\n**Methods used:**\n- Cross-instruction classification probes (10-way, per layer)\n- Per-layer attention profiling (instruction-dominant heads)\n- R² action probes with ridge regression + MCCV (n=1000, demonstration-level splits)\n- Counterfactual perturbation (wrong-task, shuffled, random, empty instructions) with Cohen's d / cosine distance\n- RSA and CKA for geometric analysis\n- Factorial design: trained/pretrained/untrained × with/without instruction × token position (image_mean, last_preaction, instruction)\n- Weight divergence analysis (frozen encoder verification)\n- Cross-benchmark replication (Goal vs Spatial)\n- Cross-architecture validation (3 architectures)\n\n**Key experimental tables:**\n- Table 1: RSA source decomposition (SigLIP+Proj 0.615 > VLA trained 0.488 > untrained 0.485 > DINO 0.388)\n- Table 2: Weight divergence (vision+projector IDENTICAL, LLM DIVERGED)\n- Tables 4-6: Image token instruction invariance (all Δ < 0.017)\n- Table 7: Cross-instruction probe accuracy across layers\n- Table 8: L1 attention heads (0/32 → 9/32 → 24/32 instruction-dominant)\n- Table 9-10: R² peak-exit comparison with bootstrap stats\n- Table 11: Cross-architecture exit collapse (6/6 VLM→VLA collapse, 0/4 VLA→VLA)\n- Table 14: RSA vs CKA comparison showing REVERSAL\n- Llama-2-7b-chat control showing flat R²≈0.155 (exit collapse is VLA-specific)\n\n**Key limitations the paper acknowledges:**\n1. Metric incomparability: Cohen's d (trained) vs cosine distance (untrained VLMs) — can't compare across conditions\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence for geometric claims\n3. No behavioral validation (all representational, no closed-loop rollouts)\n4. Δ>0.10 threshold is ad hoc (sensitivity analysis provided)\n5. Limited architecture diversity (all decoder-only with frozen vision encoders)\n\n**Experiment design context (from review_context.yaml):**\n- 13 planned experiments, all executed except plan_005 (orphaned, superseded)\n- CKA verification (plan_009) is the key problematic experiment: CKA ordering reverses RSA, undermining C4 geometric claims\n- R² exit collapse within OpenVLA shows Δ=0.033-0.086 (below 0.10 threshold) — the real exit collapse signal comes from TracVLA/NORA (VLM→VLA)\n- Cross-instruction probe at image_mean stays at chance (10%) confirming token isolation\n- Branch-split RSA shows DINOv2 ≈ SigLIP in fused backbone\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (methods and experiments focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (methods and experiments focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Soundness (1-4), Significance (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Statistical methodology (MCCV, bootstrap, effect sizes)\n- Whether the Δ>0.10 threshold is principled\n- The metric incomparability issue (Cohen's d vs cosine distance)\n- RSA/CKA divergence and what it means for claims\n- Whether cross-architecture validation is convincing\n- Whether the experimental design adequately supports the claims\n- The chat-finetuned control experiment\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific tables, numbers, and sections."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1634,"cache_read_input_tokens":63649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1634},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"d000ba9d-8135-4d58-8550-6a15a71ac677"}
