as metric-dependent observation, not a robust finding.\n\n**Methods used:**\n- Cross-instruction classification probes (10-way, per layer)\n- Per-layer attention profiling (instruction-dominant heads)\n- R² action probes with ridge regression + MCCV (n=1000, demonstration-level splits)\n- Counterfactual perturbation (wrong-task, shuffled, random, empty instructions) with Cohen's d / cosine distance\n- RSA and CKA for geometric analysis\n- Factorial design: trained/pretrained/untrained × with/without instruction × token position (image_mean, last_preaction, instruction)\n- Weight divergence analysis (frozen encoder verification)\n- Cross-benchmark replication (Goal vs Spatial)\n- Cross-architecture validation (3 architectures)\n\n**Key experimental tables:**\n- Table 1: RSA source decomposition (SigLIP+Proj 0.615 > VLA trained 0.488 > untrained 0.485 > DINO 0.388)\n- Table 2: Weight divergence (vision+projector IDENTICAL, LLM DIVERGED)\n- Tables 4-6: Image token instruction invariance (all Δ < 0.017)\n- Table 7: Cross-instruction probe accuracy across layers\n- Table 8: L1 attention heads (0/32 → 9/32 → 24/32 instruction-dominant)\n- Table 9-10: R² peak-exit comparison with bootstrap stats\n- Table 11: Cross-architecture exit collapse (6/6 VLM→VLA collapse, 0/4 VLA→VLA)\n- Table 14: RSA vs CKA comparison showing REVERSAL\n- Llama-2-7b-chat control showing flat R²≈0.155 (exit collapse is VLA-specific)\n\n**Key limitations the paper acknowledges:**\n1. Metric incomparability: Cohen's d (trained) vs cosine distance (untrained VLMs) — can't compare across conditions\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence for geometric claims\n3. No behavioral validation (all representational, no closed-loop rollouts)\n4. Δ>0.10 threshold is ad hoc (sensitivity analysis provided)\n5. Limited architecture diversity (all decoder-only with frozen vision encoders)\n\n**Experiment design context (from review_context.yaml):**\n- 13 planned experiments, all executed except plan_005 (orphaned, superseded)\n- CKA verification (plan_009) is the key problematic experiment: CKA ordering reverses RSA, undermining C4 geometric claims\n- R² exit collapse within OpenVLA shows Δ=0.033-0.086 (below 0.10 threshold) — the real exit collapse signal comes from TracVLA/NORA (VLM→VLA)\n- Cross-instruction probe at image_mean stays at chance (10%) confirming token isolation\n- Branch-split RSA shows DINOv2 ≈ SigLIP in fused backbone\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (methods and experiments focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (methods and experiments focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Soundness (1-4), Significance (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Statistical methodology (MCCV, bootstrap, effect sizes)\n- Whether the Δ>0.10 threshold is principled\n- The metric incomparability issue (Cohen's d vs cosine distance)\n- RSA/CKA divergence and what it means for claims\n- Whether cross-architecture validation is convincing\n- Whether the experimental design adequately supports the claims\n- The chat-finetuned control experiment\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific tables, numbers, and sections."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"63d07024-34cd-4129-bd4f-c156a4488c20","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:26:33.219Z"}
