{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AVC1BXzqexNNxWamGuDcGZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe investigate how action-relevant representations are organized across three VLA architectures using complementary probing, attention, and perturbation analyses.\n5\t\n6\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n7\t\\subsection{Architectures and Data}\n8\t\\label{sec:arch_data}\n9\t\n10\tWe study three VLAs that span distinct backbone families (Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview}).\n11\t\\textbf{OpenVLA}~\\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} chains a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2} (DINOv2 ViT-L/14 + SigLIP-SO400M), an MLP projector, and Llama-2-7B~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} (6.74B parameters, 32 layers, 32 multi-head attention [MHA] heads) for autoregressive 7-D action decoding.\n12\tIt is pretrained on Open X-Embodiment~\\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} (OXE; 970K episodes across multiple robot platforms).\n13\t\\textbf{TracVLA}~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} pairs a SigLIP encoder with Phi-3-mini~\\cite{abdin2024phi3} (3.8B parameters, 32 layers, 32 MHA heads).\n14\tTracVLA is trained on an OXE subset with spatial trace conditioning.\n15\t\\textbf{NORA}~\\cite{hung2025nora} uses Qwen2.5-VL-3B ($\\sim$3B parameters, 36 layers, grouped-query attention [GQA] with 16 query heads and 2 KV heads) with the native Qwen2.5-VL vision encoder, which processes images at dynamic resolution without a separate projection stage.\n16\t\n17\tEach architecture is evaluated in trained and reference conditions; we define the \\emph{reference checkpoint} as the corresponding pretrained model before LIBERO fine-tuning.\n18\tA critical asymmetry distinguishes the reference baselines: the OpenVLA reference is an OXE-pretrained VLA, already fine-tuned for action prediction on 970K episodes, while the TracVLA and NORA references are pure vision-language models (Phi-3-Vision and Qwen2.5-VL) with no action training.\n19\tOpenVLA trained-vs-reference comparisons therefore measure a VLA-to-VLA transition; TracVLA and NORA comparisons measure VLM-to-VLA transitions.\n20\tFor OpenVLA, two additional conditions support deep-dive analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}): the Llama-2 base language model and a random-weight initialization.\n21\t\n22\tWe evaluate on LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023_libero}, comprising four task suites (Goal, Spatial, Object, Long-Horizon) with 10 tasks and 50 demonstrations each.\n23\tThe three architectures and four suites form a $3 \\times 4$ matrix; 10 of 12 cells are filled (NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints).\n24\t\n25\tArchitecture details appear in Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview} (Appendix).\n26\t\n27\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n28\t\\subsection{Representation Extraction}\n29\tFrom each architecture, we extract hidden states at every transformer layer from two token positions: (a)~\\textbf{image\\_mean}, the mean activation over image tokens, and (b)~\\textbf{readout}, the hidden state at the final pre-action token, which follows both image and instruction tokens in the causal sequence and can attend to all preceding context.\n30\tL0 denotes the embedding layer output, L1 through L$N$ the hidden states after each transformer layer ($N = 32$ for OpenVLA and TracVLA, $N = 36$ for NORA), with the final layer including the output layer norm.\n31\tIn all three architectures, image tokens precede instruction tokens; the causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions by construction.\n32\t\n33\tWe cross two factors in a $2 \\times 2$ factorial design: \\emph{training status} (trained vs.\\ reference) and \\emph{instruction presence} (with vs.\\ without task description), applied to all 10 available cells in the cross-architecture matrix.\n34\tOpenVLA additionally provides representations from the DinoSigLIP projector output (4{,}096-D), DINOv2 and CLIP external baselines, and the branch-split before fusion; these sources support the OpenVLA deep-dive analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).\n35\t\n36\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n37\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Analyses}\n38\t\\label{sec:cross_arch_analyses}\n39\t\n40\tThree analyses are applied to every available cell in the $3 \\times 4$ matrix.\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Action probes.}\n43\tTo quantify how much action-relevant structure is accessible at each layer, we train two-layer MLP probes~\\cite{alain2017} to predict 7-DoF action vectors from frozen representations (architecture and training details in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_methods_appendix}).\n44\tWe report probe $R^2$ as a measure of action decodability.\n45\tHigher $R^2$ indicates that actions are more accurately predictable from that representation under the probe; it should not be interpreted as evidence that the model causally uses that layer for action generation.\n46\tWe define $\\Delta R^2$ as the difference between the maximum layer-wise $R^2$ and the exit-layer $R^2$, and use $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ as a descriptive threshold for substantial peak-to-exit reduction.\n47\tTrain/test splits follow demonstration boundaries (demo-level splitting reduces $R^2$ from $0.7$--$0.9$ to $0.4$--$0.6$ vs.\\ random splitting; Appendix~\\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).\n48\t95\\% CIs are obtained via Monte Carlo cross-validation ($n = 1{,}000$ iterations) with demo-level resampling; the canonical peak layer is fixed from the full-data split to avoid winner's-curse bias.\n49\tLinear probes yield consistent patterns (Appendix~\\ref{sec:linear_probe}).\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{L1 attention head profiling.}\n52\tTo determine whether attention heads preferentially route instruction information to the readout position, each L1 head is classified as instruction-dominant ($>50\\%$ of readout-token attention to instruction tokens) or image-dominant, reported as $k/N$ heads (32 for MHA, 16 for GQA).\n53\tSensitivity analysis at thresholds from $30\\%$ to $70\\%$ confirms ordering robustness (Appendix~\\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual instruction perturbation.}\n56\tTo test whether the model's actions depend on instruction content, the instruction is replaced with four conditions: empty string, wrong task, shuffled tokens, and paraphrased.\n57\tWe measure trained-model sensitivity via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE (OpenVLA, TracVLA) or cosine distance (NORA); reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations.\n58\tThese metrics operate on different scales; cross-metric ratios are invalid (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).\n59\t\n60\tOpenVLA additionally supports cross-instruction probes, representational similarity analysis (RSA)~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008}, and external baselines (DINOv2~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2}, CLIP~\\cite{radford2021_clip}); these single-architecture methods are detailed in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_methods_appendix}.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"95ee6895-3253-4038-8f8c-202777d7358b","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:26:54.352Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SXefn8PqE3eAiFtfrpJphs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Udkvd6tP7ii59n69r2Tn6P","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/docs/paper/experiments.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1950,"cache_read_input_tokens":21961,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1950,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"b14f14ee-50be-4892-899a-e33b5ddb1bae"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Udkvd6tP7ii59n69r2Tn6P","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n5\t\\label{sec:setup}\n6\t\n7\tWe apply all three analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}) to the $3 \\times 4$ architecture-suite matrix described in \\S\\ref{sec:arch_data} (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n8\tOf the 12 cells, 10 are filled; NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints.\n9\tFor OpenVLA LIBERO-Goal, $N = 63{,}228$ observations; other cells are at comparable scale.\n10\tFor trained models that produce action outputs, counterfactual sensitivity is measured via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE.\n11\tFor reference VLMs (Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) that do not produce actions, sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations; these metrics operate on different scales and are not directly comparable (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).\n12\tOpenVLA's OXE-pretrained checkpoint produces actions, permitting same-metric comparison (Figure~\\ref{fig:result_matrix}; Bonferroni correction in Appendix~\\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).\n13\t\n14\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n15\t\\centering\n16\t\\includegraphics[width=0.82\\textwidth]{figures/fig_matrix_heatmap.pdf}\n17\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n18\t\\caption{\\textbf{Cross-architecture evaluation matrix.} Each cell reports $\\Delta R^2$ (peak$-$exit). OpenVLA: VLA-to-VLA; TracVLA and NORA: VLM-to-VLA. Gray cells: checkpoints not released by original authors. Red borders indicate $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$.}\n19\t\\label{fig:result_matrix}\n20\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n21\t\\end{figure}\n22\t\n23\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n24\t\\subsection{OpenVLA: Motivating Single-Architecture Analysis}\n25\t\\label{sec:openvla_routing}\n26\t\n27\tBefore testing cross-architectural generality, we establish three core phenomena on OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla} (full analysis in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_deepdive_appendix}).\n28\tAt L1, three conditions form a gradient: random-weight initialization allocates 0/32 instruction-dominant heads, Llama-2 base~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} allocates 9/32, and VLA-trained allocates 24/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning is associated with instruction routing beyond language-model priors.\n29\tCounterfactual perturbation produces graded action divergence ordered by semantic distance ($d = 0.81$--$1.18$), and dose-response ablation of instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).\n30\t$R^2$ probing localizes training effects at the readout token ($+0.069$ vs.\\ $+0.015$ at image\\_mean), and readout instruction classification remains at $100\\%$ through L31 in the trained model (reference: $82\\%$ decay).\n31\tThese phenomena motivate the cross-architecture tests that follow.\n32\t\n33\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n34\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n35\t\\subsection{Exit Collapse: Layer-Wise Action Probing}\n36\t\\label{sec:r2_collapse}\n37\t\n38\tWe define $R^2$ exit collapse as $\\Delta R^2 = R^2_{\\text{peak}} - R^2_{\\text{exit}}$, the drop in action-probe decodability from peak layer to exit layer (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n39\tVLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial exit collapse across all tested conditions: TracVLA~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} $\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ across all four suites, and NORA~\\cite{hung2025nora} $\\Delta R^2 = 0.308$ (Spatial) and $0.455$ (Goal) (Figure~\\ref{fig:r2_collapse}), all exceeding the $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ threshold for substantive collapse (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}).\n40\tUnder VLA-to-VLA conditions, OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla} shows only small fluctuations across all four suites ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.033$--$0.086$, all below $0.10$); reference baselines similarly show $\\Delta R^2 \\leq 0.097$.\n41\tThe gap between the largest non-collapse value ($0.097$) and smallest collapse value ($0.156$) spans $0.059$; any threshold in this interval yields identical grouping, and a permutation test rejects random label assignment ($p = 0.008$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).\n42\tEven excluding NORA-Goal (whose bootstrap CI is unreliable; Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}, footnote $b$), the VLM-to-VLA range remains $\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ (TracVLA only), consistently exceeding the $0.10$ threshold.\n43\tMonte Carlo cross-validation supports this separation: VLM-to-VLA trained CIs lie entirely above zero (lower bounds $\\geq 0.09$), while VLA-to-VLA trained CIs span or nearly span zero (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n44\t\n45\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n46\t\\centering\n47\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_r2_collapse.pdf}\n48\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n49\t\\caption{\\textbf{Layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ (LIBERO-Goal, readout token).} Solid: trained; dashed: reference. VLM-to-VLA transitions show pronounced peak-to-exit drops ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$); VLA-to-VLA remains flat ($< 0.09$). Quantitative $\\Delta R^2$ values use demo-level splitting (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}); see Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix} for all cells.}\n50\t\\label{fig:r2_collapse}\n51\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n52\t\\end{figure}\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}\n55\tTo test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models that share backbones with our VLAs but lack action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat (OpenVLA), Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (TracVLA), and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (NORA).\\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules; see Appendix~\\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}\n56\tAll three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; $R^2 = 0.155 \\pm 0.001$, $0.155 \\pm 0.000$, and $0.155 \\pm 0.000$ respectively; Appendix~\\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}, Figure~\\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).\n57\tThe absence of exit collapse in any NLP control is inconsistent with exit collapse being a generic consequence of language-model fine-tuning.\n58\t\\textbf{In sum, exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}\n59\t\n60\t\\begin{table}[t]\n61\t\\centering\n62\t\\caption{\\textbf{Peak-to-exit action-probe drop across the architecture-suite matrix.} $\\Delta R^2$ is computed as the difference between the maximum layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ and the exit-layer $R^2$. Values above 0.10 are treated as substantial descriptive drops. OpenVLA is a VLA-to-VLA comparison, whereas TracVLA and NORA are VLM-to-VLA comparisons.}\n63\t\\label{tab:cross_arch_matrix}\n64\t\\small\n65\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n66\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll cccc@{}}\n67\t\\toprule\n68\t& & & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{$\\Delta R^2$ (peak $-$ exit)} \\\\\n69\t\\cmidrule(l){4-7}\n70\tArchitecture & Suite & Transition & Trained & 95\\% CI & Reference & 95\\% CI \\\\\n71\t\\midrule\n72\tOpenVLA & Goal    & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.086 & [$-$0.060, 0.110] & 0.056 & [0.010, 0.080] \\\\\n73\tOpenVLA & Spatial & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.010, 0.100]    & 0.044 & [0.030, 0.110] \\\\\n74\tOpenVLA & Object  & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.000, 0.090]    & 0.051 & [0.000, 0.090] \\\\\n75\tOpenVLA & Long    & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.033 & [0.000, 0.100]    & 0.035 & [0.030, 0.100] \\\\\n76\t\\midrule\n77\tTracVLA & Goal    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.333 & [0.210, 0.450]    & 0.065 & [$-$0.110, 0.050] \\\\\n78\tTracVLA & Spatial & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.156 & [0.090, 0.220]    & 0.048 & ---$^{a}$ \\\\\n79\tTracVLA & Object  & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.362 & [0.280, 0.500]    & 0.009 & [$-$0.070, 0.020] \\\\\n80\tTracVLA & Long    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.358 & [0.170, 0.530]    & 0.097 & [$-$0.020, 0.110] \\\\\n81\t\\midrule\n82\tNORA    & Goal    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.455$^{b}$ & ---$^{b}$  & 0.081 & [$-$0.010, 0.110] \\\\\n83\tNORA    & Spatial & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.308 & [0.300, 0.510]    & 0.089 & [$-$0.010, 0.090] \\\\\n84\t\\bottomrule\n85\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize NORA Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints are unavailable.}\\\\\n86\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$CI artifact from flat $R^2$ profile. $^{b}$Bootstrap CI unreliable (exit-layer $R^2 \\approx -0.006$).}\\\\\n87\t\\end{tabular}\n88\t\\end{table}\n89\t\n90\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n92\t\\subsection{Instruction Sensitivity: Counterfactual Perturbation}\n93\t\\label{sec:cf_sensitivity}\n94\t\n95\t\\subsubsection{Instruction Sensitivity in Action-Producing VLAs}\n96\tWe first evaluate counterfactual sensitivity in action-producing models using a shared action-space metric.\n97\tAcross OpenVLA and TracVLA, instruction perturbations produce medium-to-large action divergence in all eight architecture-suite cells (Figure~\\ref{fig:counterfactual_bar}).\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\\columnwidth}\n100\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n101\t\\centering\n102\t\\includegraphics[width=0.44\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_counterfactual_bar.pdf}\n103\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n104\t\\caption{Counterfactual sensitivity across the architecture-suite matrix. (a)~Cohen's $d$ for four perturbation conditions (E=empty, W=wrong, S=shuffled, P=paraphrased); asterisks mark the top condition per cell; dashed line: large-effect threshold ($d = 0.8$). (b)~Cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale).}\n105\t\\label{fig:counterfactual_bar}\n106\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n107\t\\end{wrapfigure}\n108\tEvery perturbation condition yields $d = 0.45$--$1.52$ (Table~\\ref{tab:cf_summary}; full per-condition rankings in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:counterfactual}).\n109\tThe ``empty'' condition ranks first in 6 of 8 cells ($d = 1.17$--$1.52$); ``paraphrased'' ranks last in 7 of 8, indicating that VLAs encode instruction content beyond token-level matching.\n110\tTracVLA produces systematically higher effect sizes ($d = 0.97$--$1.52$) than OpenVLA ($0.45$--$1.21$).\n111\tRemoving the instruction entirely often produces larger divergence than replacing it with a wrong task, consistent with instruction removal producing larger disruption than instruction substitution.\n112\t\n113\tTwo OpenVLA cells permit same-metric trained/reference comparison: on Spatial, training increases sensitivity (trained$-$reference difference: $+0.05$ to $+0.24$ across conditions); on Long-Horizon, the OXE-pretrained model exceeds the LIBERO-trained model ($d = 1.05$ vs.\\ $0.57$), consistent with the general-purpose model maintaining broader instruction responsiveness.\n114\t\\textbf{Instruction perturbation produces graded action divergence across all tested conditions, with effect sizes reflecting semantic distance.}\n115\t\n116\t\\begin{table}[t]\n117\t\\centering\n118\t\\caption{\\textbf{Counterfactual sensitivity summary.} Cohen's $d$ range and most sensitive perturbation for action-producing models (OpenVLA, TracVLA). NORA uses cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale; see \\S\\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\n119\t\\label{tab:cf_summary}\n120\t\\small\n121\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcl@{}}\n122\t\\toprule\n123\tArchitecture & $d$ range & Most sensitive & Metric \\\\\n124\t\\midrule\n125\tOpenVLA & 0.45--1.21 & empty (2/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n126\tTracVLA & 0.97--1.52 & empty (4/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\tNORA    & 0.28--sat. & --- & cosine dist.$^{a}$ \\\\\n129\t\\bottomrule\n130\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$Representation-space only; not comparable to Cohen's $d$ (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\\\\\n131\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize Empty ranks first in 6/8 suites total across both architectures.}\\\\\n132\t\\end{tabular}\n133\t\\end{table}\n134\t\n135\t\\subsubsection{Representation-Space Sensitivity in NORA and Reference VLMs}\n136\t\\label{sec:cf_repr}\n137\t\n138\tNORA and reference VLMs lack action outputs; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale from Cohen's $d$).\n139\tNORA Goal produces saturated cosine distances ($1.0$); NORA Spatial peaks at $0.275$; reference VLMs show weak sensitivity ($\\leq 0.19$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:cf_details_appendix}).\n140\tThe trained-reference contrast is consistent with action-space findings, though the different metric prevents direct magnitude comparison.\n141\t\n142\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n143\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n144\t\\subsection{Inherited Routing: L1 Attention Profiling}\n145\t\\label{sec:l1_priors}\n146\t\n147\tL1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures reveal a pattern that contrasts with the training-associated effects documented in \\S\\ref{sec:r2_collapse} and \\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.\n148\tThe TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) allocates 32/32 heads to instruction-dominant attention across all four suites, saturating the capacity of its 32-head L1 before any VLA training.\n149\tTraining slightly reduces this count to 27--30/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning does not increase L1 instruction-dominant attention in this architecture.\n150\tThe NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) allocates 13--14 of its 16 L1 heads to instruction dominance (81--88\\%), and training leaves this essentially unchanged at 12--13/16.\n151\tOpenVLA shows 24--26/32 instruction-dominant heads in the OXE-pretrained baseline; trained values are similar (14--24/32), with the largest change in Long-Horizon where training reduces the count from 25 to 14 heads (Figure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}).\n152\t\n153\tIf VLA training drove L1 instruction routing, reference models should show low counts that increase after training, as in OpenVLA's random-weight condition (0/32; \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}); instead, both reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate, and training leaves counts unchanged or slightly reduced.\n154\t\\textbf{L1 instruction routing is an inherited backbone property, not a consequence of VLA training.}\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\\columnwidth}\n157\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n158\t\\centering\n159\t\\includegraphics[width=0.44\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_attention.pdf}\n160\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n161\t\\caption{L1 instruction-dominant head counts (trained vs.\\ reference; heads with $>50\\%$ attention on instruction tokens). Reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate; training does not increase counts. Medians across available LIBERO suites per architecture.}\n162\t\\label{fig:cross_arch_l1}\n163\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n164\t\\end{wrapfigure}\n165\t\n166\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n167\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n168\t\\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\\ Inherited Routing}\n169\t\\label{sec:routing_synthesis}\n170\t\n171\tThe three analyses separate two empirical patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from reference backbones.\n172\tDespite qualitatively different reference starting points (OpenVLA: peak at L0 with monotonic decline; Phi-3-Vision: gradual decline from L6), trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($R^2 = 0.637$ at L9 vs.\\ $0.634$ at L11, $\\Delta = 0.003$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}, Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview}), suggesting that action fine-tuning is associated with convergence toward a shared mid-layer structure regardless of starting point.\n173\tThese probes measure decodability, not causal reliance (label-permutation controls confirm genuine structure; Appendix~\\ref{sec:label_permutation}).\n174\tMatched-size ablation reveals that instruction sensitivity is distributed across heads in proportion to their instruction attention fraction, consistent with instruction information being woven into the general residual stream rather than concentrated in a dedicated routing circuit ($d = 0.65$ vs.\\ $0.60$, 8\\% gap; Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:specificity_control}).\n175\tThis distributed pattern suggests that uniform pruning may be preferable to attention-score-based head selection for VLA compression.\n176\tThe consistent three-way pattern provides a first cross-architecture decomposition of VLA instruction routing.\n177\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"a0cd52d2-19a3-4b15-ac3d-e22a2ae2a399","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:26:54.443Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VvfcTWFfCNeSNGEzVqowPu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018pWsj3F71Y5fPHh353Y6H9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (OpenReview, ~32% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nConfidence: 1 (low) to 5 (high)\n\nCoRL requires relevance to robot learning. Papers must include a limitations section; treat honestly reported limitations kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\nThis is an analysis/interpretability paper (not a new model). It studies how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing at the token level. It examines 3 architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. **Routing reorganization:** VLA training shifts instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U to entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern. 24/32 heads instruction-dominant at L1 (vs 9/32 pretrained).\n2. **R² exit collapse:** One-time reorganization at VLM→VLA boundary (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455), absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells).\n3. **Three-tier routing gradient:** VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n4. **Vision encoder geometry (qualified observation):** Non-monotonic RSA profile, but CKA reverses the ordering. Explicitly acknowledged as metric-dependent.\n\n**Paper structure:**\n- §1 Intro: frames the problem as \"how does VLA training reshape instruction routing\"\n- §2 Related Work: 3 short paragraphs (VLA models, representation analysis in transformers, instruction following in LMs)\n- §3 Experimental Framework: models/data, 4 analysis methods (cross-instruction probe, attention profiling, R² action probe, counterfactual perturbation), RSA/CKA, factorial design\n- §4 Token-Level Specialization: frozen encoder dominance, DINOv2 baseline, instruction invariance of image tokens (3 controls)\n- §5 Instruction Routing Reorganization: three routing strategies, L1 attention reorganization, R² exit collapse, cross-architecture validation, counterfactual sensitivity, per-layer profiles\n- §6 Cross-Benchmark Replication\n- §7 Vision Encoder Geometry: RSA vs CKA divergence, cross-encoder comparison, fused backbone decomposition\n- §8 Discussion: what VLA training does, three-tier gradient, limitations (5 items)\n- §9 Conclusion\n\n**Key limitations acknowledged:**\n1. Metric incomparability (Cohen's d vs cosine distance across conditions)\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence\n3. No behavioral validation\n4. Ad hoc Δ>0.10 threshold\n5. All decoder-only with frozen vision encoders\n\n**Related work landscape you should consider:**\n- Representation analysis in transformers: probing classifiers (Belinkov & Glass 2019), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019)\n- VLA models: RT-2 (Brohan et al. 2023), OpenVLA (Kim et al. 2024), TracVLA, NORA\n- Instruction following in LLMs: instruction tuning analysis (Wei et al., Wang et al.)\n- Behavioral VLA analysis: studies showing visual dominance and instruction underutilization\n- Mechanistic interpretability: circuit analysis, causal tracing, activation patching\n- Information flow in transformers: residual stream analysis, attention knockout\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Novelty (1-4), Clarity (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Is this genuinely novel or incremental application of known interpretability tools to VLAs?\n- How does this compare to existing mechanistic interpretability work on multimodal models?\n- Is the related work section adequate? What's missing?\n- Does the paper offer actionable insights for VLA design, or is it purely descriptive?\n- Is the \"routing reorganization\" framing genuinely new vs. obvious from architecture?\n- How does the instruction invariance finding (§4.3) relate to prior work on causal masking?\n- Is the cross-architecture validation truly diverse enough?\n- Does the paper's scope match CoRL's focus on robot learning?\n- Is the claim about \"diagnostic signatures\" substantiated?\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific sections, claims, and numbers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1634,"cache_read_input_tokens":63649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1634},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"c7aa9a01-2c36-425f-aaae-509d74a9335f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a6a4cb896c7677648","tool_use_id":"toolu_018pWsj3F71Y5fPHh353Y6H9","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (OpenReview, ~32% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nConfidence: 1 (low) to 5 (high)\n\nCoRL requires relevance to robot learning. Papers must include a limitations section; treat honestly reported limitations kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\nThis is an analysis/interpretability paper (not a new model). It studies how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing at the token level. It examines 3 architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. **Routing reorganization:** VLA training shifts instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U to entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern. 24/32 heads instruction-dominant at L1 (vs 9/32 pretrained).\n2. **R² exit collapse:** One-time reorganization at VLM→VLA boundary (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455), absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells).\n3. **Three-tier routing gradient:** VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n4. **Vision encoder geometry (qualified observation):** Non-monotonic RSA profile, but CKA reverses the ordering. Explicitly acknowledged as metric-dependent.\n\n**Paper structure:**\n- §1 Intro: frames the problem as \"how does VLA training reshape instruction routing\"\n- §2 Related Work: 3 short paragraphs (VLA models, representation analysis in transformers, instruction following in LMs)\n- §3 Experimental Framework: models/data, 4 analysis methods (cross-instruction probe, attention profiling, R² action probe, counterfactual perturbation), RSA/CKA, factorial design\n- §4 Token-Level Specialization: frozen encoder dominance, DINOv2 baseline, instruction invariance of image tokens (3 controls)\n- §5 Instruction Routing Reorganization: three routing strategies, L1 attention reorganization, R² exit collapse, cross-architecture validation, counterfactual sensitivity, per-layer profiles\n- §6 Cross-Benchmark Replication\n- §7 Vision Encoder Geometry: RSA vs CKA divergence, cross-encoder comparison, fused backbone decomposition\n- §8 Discussion: what VLA training does, three-tier gradient, limitations (5 items)\n- §9 Conclusion\n\n**Key limitations acknowledged:**\n1. Metric incomparability (Cohen's d vs cosine distance across conditions)\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence\n3. No behavioral validation\n4. Ad hoc Δ>0.10 threshold\n5. All decoder-only with frozen vision encoders\n\n**Related work landscape you should consider:**\n- Representation analysis in transformers: probing classifiers (Belinkov & Glass 2019), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019)\n- VLA models: RT-2 (Brohan et al. 2023), OpenVLA (Kim et al. 2024), TracVLA, NORA\n- Instruction following in LLMs: instruction tuning analysis (Wei et al., Wang et al.)\n- Behavioral VLA analysis: studies showing visual dominance and instruction underutilization\n- Mechanistic interpretability: circuit analysis, causal tracing, activation patching\n- Information flow in transformers: residual stream analysis, attention knockout\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Novelty (1-4), Clarity (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Is this genuinely novel or incremental application of known interpretability tools to VLAs?\n- How does this compare to existing mechanistic interpretability work on multimodal models?\n- Is the related work section adequate? What's missing?\n- Does the paper offer actionable insights for VLA design, or is it purely descriptive?\n- Is the \"routing reorganization\" framing genuinely new vs. obvious from architecture?\n- How does the instruction invariance finding (§4.3) relate to prior work on causal masking?\n- Is the cross-architecture validation truly diverse enough?\n- Does the paper's scope match CoRL's focus on robot learning?\n- Is the claim about \"diagnostic signatures\" substantiated?\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific sections, claims, and numbers.","uuid":"72705e06-8475-4202-8bec-854976b65947","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (OpenReview, ~32% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nConfidence: 1 (low) to 5 (high)\n\nCoRL requires relevance to robot learning. Papers must include a limitations section; treat honestly reported limitations kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\nThis is an analysis/interpretability paper (not a new model). It studies how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing at the token level. It examines 3 architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. **Routing reorganization:** VLA training shifts instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U to entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern. 24/32 heads instruction-dominant at L1 (vs 9/32 pretrained).\n2. **R² exit collapse:** One-time reorganization at VLM→VLA boundary (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455), absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells).\n3. **Three-tier routing gradient:** VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n4. **Vision encoder geometry (qualified observation):** Non-monotonic RSA profile, but CKA reverses the ordering. Explicitly acknowledged as metric-dependent.\n\n**Paper structure:**\n- §1 Intro: frames the problem as \"how does VLA training reshape instruction routing\"\n- §2 Related Work: 3 short paragraphs (VLA models, representation analysis in transformers, instruction following in LMs)\n- §3 Experimental Framework: models/data, 4 analysis methods (cross-instruction probe, attention profiling, R² action probe, counterfactual perturbation), RSA/CKA, factorial design\n- §4 Token-Level Specialization: frozen encoder dominance, DINOv2 baseline, instruction invariance of image tokens (3 controls)\n- §5 Instruction Routing Reorganization: three routing strategies, L1 attention reorganization, R² exit collapse, cross-architecture validation, counterfactual sensitivity, per-layer profiles\n- §6 Cross-Benchmark Replication\n- §7 Vision Encoder Geometry: RSA vs CKA divergence, cross-encoder comparison, fused backbone decomposition\n- §8 Discussion: what VLA training does, three-tier gradient, limitations (5 items)\n- §9 Conclusion\n\n**Key limitations acknowledged:**\n1. Metric incomparability (Cohen's d vs cosine distance across conditions)\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence\n3. No behavioral validation\n4. Ad hoc Δ>0.10 threshold\n5. All decoder-only with frozen vision encoders\n\n**Related work landscape you should consider:**\n- Representation analysis in transformers: probing classifiers (Belinkov & Glass 2019), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019)\n- VLA models: RT-2 (Brohan et al. 2023), OpenVLA (Kim et al. 2024), TracVLA, NORA\n- Instruction following in LLMs: instruction tuning analysis (Wei et al., Wang et al.)\n- Behavioral VLA analysis: studies showing visual dominance and instruction underutilization\n- Mechanistic interpretability: circuit analysis, causal tracing, activation patching\n- Information flow in transformers: residual stream analysis, attention knockout\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Novelty (1-4), Clarity (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Is this genuinely novel or incremental application of known interpretability tools to VLAs?\n- How does this compare to existing mechanistic interpretability work on multimodal models?\n- Is the related work section adequate? What's missing?\n- Does the paper offer actionable insights for VLA design, or is it purely descriptive?\n- Is the \"routing reorganization\" framing genuinely new vs. obvious from architecture?\n- How does the instruction invariance finding (§4.3) relate to prior work on causal masking?\n- Is the cross-architecture validation truly diverse enough?\n- Does the paper's scope match CoRL's focus on robot learning?\n- Is the claim about \"diagnostic signatures\" substantiated?\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific sections, claims, and numbers."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018pWsj3F71Y5fPHh353Y6H9","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"2ad86c43-e278-4291-95c8-09f0b47bc7fd","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:26:54.963Z"}
