{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AL5D8GZH4KWykrg1ix7bwa","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[h]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Mean absolute action shift per dimension under L1 head ablation. Instruction: 24 dominant heads; Control: 8 remaining heads. Gripper shift dominates both conditions.}\n307\t\\label{tab:action_shift_perdim}\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\tCondition & $dx$ & $dy$ & $dz$ & $rx$ & $ry$ & $rz$ & Gripper \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\tInstruction (24) & 0.002 & 0.005 & 0.008 & 0.021 & 0.031 & 0.034 & 0.291 \\\\\n314\tControl (8) & 0.000 & 0.001 & 0.002 & 0.008 & 0.014 & 0.018 & 0.141 \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\end{table}\n318\t\n319\t\\subsection{Pretrained LLM Representation Quality Under Visual Input}\n320\tA potential concern with the three-condition comparison (\\S\\ref{sec:arch_data}) is that the pretrained Llama-2 backbone never encountered projected visual tokens during pretraining, so its representations of such input could be degenerate.\n321\tWe evaluate three diagnostics to assess representation quality.\n322\tActivation magnitude grows from $16.5$ (L0) to $90.2$ (L31) for Llama-2 base, compared to $16.5 \\to 51.7$ for the VLA-trained model; the pretrained LLM produces larger but non-exploding activations with no evidence of collapse.\n323\tPer-layer cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers decreases progressively from $1.0$ to $\\approx 0.28$ at L25, consistent with gradual differentiation rather than catastrophic out-of-distribution behavior.\n324\tTop-10 principal component variance explained remains stable across all layers ($0.59$--$0.74$), with no low-rank collapse.\n325\tThese diagnostics indicate that the pretrained LLM produces structured, full-rank representations of visual input despite never encountering projected visual tokens during pretraining (Figure~\\ref{fig:ood_diagnostics}). These results support the validity of the three-condition comparison.\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_ood_diagnostics.pdf}\n330\t\\caption{Pretrained LLM representation diagnostics under visual input. Left: activation magnitude across layers (Llama-2 base vs.\\ VLA-trained). Center: cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers. Right: top-10 PC variance explained. All three metrics indicate structured, non-degenerate representations.}\n331\t\\label{fig:ood_diagnostics}\n332\t\\end{figure}\n333\t\n334\t\\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}\n335\t\\label{sec:linear_probe}\n336\t\n337\tTo rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\\text{params} \\approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \\to 7$, $N/\\text{params} \\approx 2.2$).\n338\tThe inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while the reference declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).\n339\tThe trained--reference gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\\Delta = +0.015$); trained representations are thus more probe-decodable for action information.\n340\tReference deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$); MLP nonlinearity partially rescues these degraded representations.\n341\tFor readout tokens, the linear probe also corroborates the inverted-U (peak L13, $R^2 = 0.596$), with the same peak layer as the MLP probe and a trained-vs-reference gap of $\\Delta = 0.103$ ($p < 0.001$).\n342\tThese results indicate that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts (Figure~\\ref{fig:linear_vs_mlp}).\n343\t\n344\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n345\t\\centering\n346\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_linear_vs_mlp_probe.pdf}\n347\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ for linear (dashed) and MLP (solid) probes across trained (blue) and reference (red) conditions. The inverted-U reproduces under both probe architectures; the trained--reference gap is larger for linear probes.}\n348\t\\label{fig:linear_vs_mlp}\n349\t\\end{figure}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Functional Probe Comparison}\n352\tFigure~\\ref{fig:probe_bars} compares functional probe $R^2$ across representation sources at each source's best layer.\n353\t\n354\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n355\t\\centering\n356\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.pdf}\n357\t\\caption{Functional probe $R^2$ for action prediction (best layer per source). Trained readout ($R^2{=}0.678$, L13) and trained image\\_mean ($0.655$, L8) exceed the projector baseline ($0.630$); the inverted-U extends to functional capacity.}\n358\t\\label{fig:probe_bars}\n359\t\\end{figure}\n360\t\n361\t\\subsection{Training Effect and Instruction Invariance}\n362\tFigure~\\ref{fig:factorial} shows trained vs.\\ reference RSA profiles and instruction invariance.\n363\tInstruction invariance holds across all conditions: maximum $|\\Delta| < 0.006$, so action geometry is visually grounded and not modulated by language instructions.\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig5.pdf}\n368\t\\caption{\\textbf{(a)}~Trained vs.\\ reference image\\_mean RSA profiles. Training shifts the peak from L0 ($0.612$) to L12 ($0.628$), yielding a modest best-vs-best gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$). \\textbf{(b)}~Instruction invariance: all conditions within $|\\Delta|{<}0.006$; action geometry is visually grounded.}\n369\t\\label{fig:factorial}\n370\t\\end{figure}\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Additional RSA Values}\n373\tDINOv2 \\texttt{patch\\_mean} achieves RSA $\\rho = 0.631$, between the \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($0.692$) and the fused DinoSigLIP ($0.634$).\n374\tVLA reference readout peaks at L1 ($\\rho = 0.626$).\n375\t\n376\t\\subsection{Cross-Suite Validation within LIBERO: Spatial}\n377\t\\label{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}\n378\t\n379\tTo assess generalization beyond in-distribution tasks, we repeat the core RSA and probe analyses on LIBERO-Spatial (10 out-of-distribution tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations each, requiring spatial reasoning).\n380\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} shows per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles for both LIBERO-Goal and LIBERO-Spatial side by side.\n381\t\n382\tThe inverted-U profile replicates on LIBERO-Spatial: trained image\\_mean RSA peaks at L14 ($\\rho = 0.691$) and $R^2$ at L13 ($0.696$), with peaks shifting ${\\sim}2$ layers deeper than LIBERO-Goal (RSA L12, $R^2$ L8--L10), consistent with increased task difficulty requiring deeper processing.\n383\tTraining produces a significant geometric gain (best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, 95\\% CI $[0.012, 0.036]$, $p < 0.001$), larger than the LIBERO-Goal effect ($\\Delta = +0.017$, CI includes zero).\n384\t\n385\tTwo notable differences are observed.\n386\tFirst, the absolute RSA values are higher on Spatial ($0.691$ vs.\\ $0.628$), possibly reflecting greater action diversity in spatial tasks that produces a more discriminative action RDM.\n387\tSecond, RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate: readout RSA peaks at L15 while $R^2$ peaks at L21 (6-layer gap), contrasting with the near-alignment on Goal ($|\\Delta_\\text{layer}| = 1$).\n388\tThis suggests that peak convergence is benchmark-specific rather than a general consequence of training.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig7.pdf}\n393\t\\caption{Cross-suite validation: per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles on LIBERO-Goal (left) vs.\\ LIBERO-Spatial (right). The inverted-U replicates across suites. LIBERO-Spatial shows a significant training effect (RSA best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$) and deeper peak layers (RSA L14, $R^2$ L13), but RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate (6-layer gap).}\n394\t\\label{fig:cross_benchmark}\n395\t\\end{figure}\n396\t\n397\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Suite Counterfactual Replication}\n398\tTable~\\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial} reports counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks).\n399\tThe ordering differs from LIBERO-Goal: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased (vs.\\ wrong $>$ empty $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased on Goal).\n400\tAll global effect sizes exceed $d = 0.8$ (per-task wrong minimum is $0.591$).\n401\tSpatial instructions differ only in prepositions (e.g., ``on the plate'' vs.\\ ``in the drawer''); the wrong-task $d = 0.885$ suggests that instruction-dominant heads encode fine-grained spatial relations, not merely task identity.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[h]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks). Ordering: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased, differing from Goal. All global $d > 0.8$.}\n406\t\\label{tab:counterfactual_spatial}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ & CV \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 1.173 & 0.087 \\\\\n413\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 0.885 & 0.272 \\\\\n414\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 0.979 & 0.162 \\\\\n415\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.807 & 0.179 \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe on LIBERO-Spatial}\n421\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial_cross_instruction} reports cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial.\n422\tThe core pattern from LIBERO-Goal replicates: image\\_mean remains at chance (${\\sim}10\\%$) across all layers and conditions, while the trained readout token saturates to $100\\%$ by L4.\n423\tThe reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal ($75.4\\%$ at L31 vs.\\ $82\\%$), consistent with greater task difficulty.\n424\t\n425\t\\begin{table}[h]\n426\t\\centering\n427\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial. Trained readout achieves $100\\%$ from L4; image\\_mean remains at chance. Reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal (cf.\\ \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).}\n428\t\\label{tab:spatial_cross_instruction}\n429\t\\small\n430\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n431\t\\toprule\n432\tToken & Condition & L1 & L4 & L8 & L16 & L31 \\\\\n433\t\\midrule\n434\treadout & Trained & 98.9\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% \\\\\n435\treadout & Reference & 99.5\\% & 98.3\\% & 85.7\\% & 68.3\\% & 75.4\\% \\\\\n436\timage\\_mean & Trained & 10.2\\% & 10.0\\% & 9.9\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.1\\% \\\\\n437\timage\\_mean & Reference & 10.1\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.2\\% \\\\\n438\t\\bottomrule\n439\t\\end{tabular}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\t\\subsection{Robustness Analyses}\n443\t\\subsubsection{Distance Metric Invariance}\n444\tThe inverted-U profile is robust to distance metric choice: cosine action distance yields identical peak layers (L12) and Pearson correlation $r > 0.989$ with the Euclidean profile reported in the main text (Table~\\ref{tab:metric_invariance}).\n445\t\n446\t\\begin{table}[h]\n447\t\\centering\n448\t\\caption{Peak layer comparison between Euclidean and cosine action distances. Profile $r$ denotes Pearson correlation across all 32 layers.}\n449\t\\label{tab:metric_invariance}\n450\t\\small\n451\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n452\t\\toprule\n453\tCondition & Eucl.\\ Peak & Cos.\\ Peak & Profile $r$ \\\\\n454\t\\midrule\n455\tTrained image\\_mean & L12 & L12 & 0.990 \\\\\n456\tTrained readout & L12 & L13 & 0.990 \\\\\n457\tReference image\\_mean & L0 & L0 & 0.995 \\\\\n458\tReference readout & L1 & L1 & 0.992 \\\\\n459\t\\bottomrule\n460\t\\end{tabular}\n461\t\\end{table}\n462\t\n463\t\\subsubsection{Per-DOF Decomposition}\n464\tRSA decomposition by action dimension indicates the inverted-U is not driven by any single degree of freedom: xyz translation (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.193$), rotation (peak L12, $\\rho = 0.175$), and gripper (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.106$) all exhibit non-monotonic profiles.\n465\tPer-DOF RSA values are lower than the 7-D RSA because single-dimension distances carry less discriminative information than the full action space.\n466\t\n467\t\\subsubsection{Permutation Test}\n468\tPermutation testing (10{,}000 shuffles) indicates all observed RSA values are highly significant: observed $\\rho$ ($0.42$--$0.63$) exceeds the null distribution maximum ($0.08$--$0.10$) by $15$--$23$ standard deviations (all $p < 0.0001$).\n469\t\n470\t\\subsubsection{Probe Seed Sensitivity}\n471\tAction probe $R^2$ is highly stable across random seeds.\n472\tOver 5 seeds (trained image\\_mean, full-layer scan), per-layer $R^2$ standard deviation is uniformly below $0.01$ (mean $0.004$), and the peak $R^2$ varies by less than $0.4\\%$ ($0.655 \\pm 0.002$; Table~\\ref{tab:seed_sensitivity}).\n473\tPeak layer concentrates in the L8--L10 region ($4/5$ seeds); the inverted-U shape is not a seed artifact.\n474\tSeed 1 peaks at L16, but with $R^2$ difference ${<}0.002$. The inverted-U plateau (L8--L16) is sufficiently flat that argmax is sensitive to minor noise while the profile shape is invariant.\n475\tA 15-seed replication confirms stability: trained peak remains at L9 ($R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.004$), with all trained/Llama-2-base layers showing CI width ${<}0.02$; the randomly initialized condition exhibits higher variance (std $0.185$ at peak), consistent with random initialization instability.\n476\tFixed-layer analysis at L9 yields $R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.008$ (trained) and $0.635 \\pm 0.011$ (Llama-2 base), confirming the inverted-U pattern.\n477\tThe per-seed peak method reports higher absolute values ($0.655$) because each seed selects its optimal layer independently; both methods agree on the qualitative pattern and the trained $>$ randomly initialized ordering.\n478\t\n479\t\\begin{table}[h]\n480\t\\centering\n481\t\\caption{Probe $R^2$ peak across 5 random seeds (trained image\\_mean).}\n482\t\\label{tab:seed_sensitivity}\n483\t\\small\n484\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n485\t\\toprule\n486\tSeed & Peak Layer & Peak $R^2$ \\\\\n487\t\\midrule\n488\t0 & L9 & 0.654 \\\\\n489\t1 & L16 & 0.656 \\\\\n490\t2 & L9 & 0.653 \\\\\n491\t3 & L10 & 0.655 \\\\\n492\t42 & L9 & 0.655 \\\\\n493\t\\midrule\n494\tMean$\\pm$std & $10.6\\pm2.7$ & $0.655\\pm0.002$ \\\\\n495\t\\bottomrule\n496\t\\end{tabular}\n497\t\\end{table}\n498\t\n499\t\\paragraph{Cross-architecture $\\Delta R^2$ seed sensitivity.}\n500\tTo verify that $\\Delta R^2$ values are not sensitive to probe initialization, we retrain MLP probes with 4 different random seeds (0, 1, 2, 42) on three representative cells.\n501\tResults show high stability: TracVLA Goal $0.227 \\pm 0.010$, TracVLA Spatial $0.136 \\pm 0.002$, OpenVLA Goal $0.013 \\pm 0.001$ (mean $\\pm$ std across seeds).\n502\tAll standard deviations are below 0.02, confirming that the exit collapse pattern ($\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ for VLM-to-VLA vs.\\ $< 0.10$ for VLA-to-VLA) is robust to probe initialization.\n503\t\n504\t\\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals for $\\Delta R^2$.}\n505\tTo assess uncertainty in individual $\\Delta R^2$ estimates, we compute 95\\% confidence intervals via 1{,}000-iteration Monte Carlo cross-validation bootstrap.\n506\tResults for 9 cells (excluding NORA-Goal due to exit-layer instability):\n507\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n508\t\\item \\textbf{VLA-to-VLA} (4 cells): CIs range from $[-0.056, 0.106]$ (widest: OpenVLA Goal) to $[0.004, 0.103]$ (OpenVLA Long).\n509\t\\item \\textbf{VLM-to-VLA} (5 cells): CIs range from $[0.091, 0.224]$ (TracVLA Spatial) to $[0.283, 0.504]$ (TracVLA Object).\n510\t\\end{itemize}\n511\tThe point-estimate gap ($0.097$ vs.\\ $0.156$, span $= 0.059$) cleanly separates the two groups.\n512\tAt the 95\\% CI level, a marginal overlap of $0.016$ exists between the upper bound of OpenVLA Goal ($0.106$) and the lower bound of TracVLA Spatial ($0.091$).\n513\tThis overlap involves only 2 of 9 cells and is driven by OpenVLA Goal's unusually wide CI (due to high variance in its exit-layer probe).\n514\tAll other cell pairs show non-overlapping CIs, confirming that the exit-collapse pattern is robust to measurement uncertainty.\n515\tA permutation test assesses whether the gap between VLM-to-VLA and VLA-to-VLA $\\Delta R^2$ values could arise by chance.\n516\tThe 9 trained-model $\\Delta R^2$ values (4 VLA-to-VLA, 5 VLM-to-VLA; NORA Goal excluded due to CI instability) are randomly reassigned to groups of size 4 and 5 ($10{,}000$ permutations); the test statistic is $\\min(\\text{VLM-to-VLA group}) - \\max(\\text{VLA-to-VLA group})$.\n517\tThe observed gap ($0.070$) is exceeded by $0.8\\%$ of permuted distributions ($p = 0.008$), indicating that the separation is unlikely to arise by chance.\n518\tNote that the permutation shuffles transition-type labels (VLM$\\to$VLA vs.\\ VLA$\\to$VLA), not trained/reference labels; all 9 values come from trained models.\n519\t\n520\t\\subsubsection{Demonstration Subsample Sensitivity}\n521\tTo verify that the inverted-U pattern is not an artifact of sample size, we subsample 25 from 50 demonstrations per task and recompute both RSA and probe profiles (Table~\\ref{tab:demo_sensitivity}).\n522\tThe RSA peak layer remains at L12 with a profile correlation of $r = 0.970$ between 50- and 25-demo conditions (high stability).\n523\tThe probe peak shifts from L9 to L11 (within the L8--L16 plateau) with $r = 0.819$; $R^2$ is more sensitive to training set size than RSA.\n524\tBoth metrics show reduced absolute values (RSA $-3.6\\%$, $R^2$ $-7.6\\%$) as expected from halved data, but the non-monotonic profile shape is preserved.\n525\t\n526\t\\begin{table}[h]\n527\t\\centering\n528\t\\caption{Inverted-U stability under demonstration subsampling (trained image\\_mean).}\n529\t\\label{tab:demo_sensitivity}\n530\t\\small\n531\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n532\t\\toprule\n533\tMetric & 50 demos & 25 demos & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n534\t\\midrule\n535\tRSA peak layer & L12 & L12 & 0 \\\\\n536\tRSA peak $\\rho$ & 0.628 & 0.606 & $-3.6\\%$ \\\\\n537\t$R^2$ peak layer & L9 & L11 & $+2$ \\\\\n538\t$R^2$ peak value & 0.659 & 0.609 & $-7.6\\%$ \\\\\n539\tRSA profile $r$ & --- & 0.970 & --- \\\\\n540\t$R^2$ profile $r$ & --- & 0.819 & --- \\\\\n541\t\\bottomrule\n542\t\\end{tabular}\n543\t\\end{table}\n544\t\n545\t\\subsubsection{Label Permutation Sanity Check}\n546\t\\label{sec:label_permutation}\n547\t\n548\tTo confirm that probe performance reflects genuine action-relevant structure rather than overfitting, we repeat the per-layer action probe with randomly shuffled labels (Figure~\\ref{fig:label_permutation}).\n549\tUnder permuted labels, Spearman $\\rho$ drops from $0.955$ to $0.001$ (trained) and from $0.933$ to $0.000$ (reference), with all 33 layers producing $|\\rho| < 0.013$.\n550\tThis establishes that the high probe accuracy reported throughout the paper is attributable to learned action-relevant representations, not probe capacity artifacts.\n551\t\n552\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n553\t\\centering\n554\t\\includegraphics[width=0.55\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_label_permutation.pdf}\n555\t\\caption{\\textbf{Label permutation control.} Action-probe Spearman $\\rho$ under real versus shuffled labels for OpenVLA trained and reference conditions. Shuffled-label $\\rho$ is indistinguishable from zero, confirming that probe performance reflects genuine representational structure. (Evaluated with random frame splitting; demo-level split yields lower absolute values but identical null-vs-real separation; see \\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}.)}\n556\t\\label{fig:label_permutation}\n557\t\\end{figure}\n558\t\n559\t\\subsubsection{Statistical Power}\n560\tPost-hoc power analysis ($N = 10$ tasks, $\\alpha = 0.05$, target power $= 0.80$) indicates minimum detectable effect sizes of $\\Delta_{\\text{RSA}} = 0.030$ and $\\Delta_{R^2} = 0.026$.\n561\tThe observed RSA training effect ($\\Delta = 0.017$, Cohen's $d_z = 0.56$) achieves power $= 0.356$, confirming the analysis is underpowered for this effect size; the non-significant RSA result (\\S\\ref{sec:routing_synthesis}) should not be interpreted as evidence of absence.\n562\tThe observed $R^2$ readout effect ($\\Delta = 0.069$, $d_z = 2.68$) achieves power $= 1.000$, confirming adequate sensitivity for the primary functional metric.\n563\t\n564\t\\subsubsection{$\\Delta R^2$ Threshold Robustness}\n565\t\\label{sec:delta_r2_threshold}\n566\t\n567\tThe main text classifies $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ as substantial exit collapse.\n568\tTable~\\ref{tab:delta_r2_threshold} shows that any threshold in the interval $(0.097, 0.156)$ yields identical three-way separation: all VLM-to-VLA trained cells above threshold, all OpenVLA trained and all reference cells below.\n569\tThis interval is bounded by the largest non-VLM-to-VLA value ($0.097$, TracVLA Long reference) and the smallest VLM-to-VLA trained value ($0.156$, TracVLA Spatial).\n570\tThe $0.10$ threshold was not selected to maximize separation; rather, it represents a natural effect-size boundary ($\\geq 10\\%$ absolute change in explained variance) that happens to fall within this gap.\n571\t\n572\t\\begin{table}[h]\n573\t\\centering\n574\t\\caption{Sensitivity of group separation to $\\Delta R^2$ threshold choice. Clean separation (all VLM-to-VLA trained above, all others below) holds for any threshold in $(0.097, 0.156)$; the 0.10 threshold is not boundary-sensitive.}\n575\t\\label{tab:delta_r2_threshold}\n576\t\\small\n577\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n578\t\\toprule\n579\tThreshold & VLM$\\to$VLA Tr. & OpenVLA Tr. & Reference & Clean \\\\\n580\t& ($N\\!/6$) & ($N\\!/4$) & ($N\\!/10$) & sep. \\\\\n581\t\\midrule\n582\t0.05 & 6/6 & 1/4 & 6/10 & No \\\\\n583\t0.08 & 6/6 & 1/4 & 3/10 & No \\\\\n584\t\\textbf{0.10} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/4} & \\textbf{0/10} & \\textbf{Yes} \\\\\n585\t\\textbf{0.15} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/4} & \\textbf{0/10} & \\textbf{Yes} \\\\\n586\t0.20 & 5/6 & 0/4 & 0/10 & No \\\\\n587\t\\bottomrule\n588\t\\end{tabular}\n589\t\\end{table}\n590\t\n591\t\\subsection{Detailed Training Effects}\n592\tTable~\\ref{tab:training_effects} reports bootstrap-based statistical comparisons of training effects on RSA and probe $R^2$.\n593\t\n594\t\\begin{table}[h]\n595\t\\centering\n596\t\\caption{Training effects on action-relevant structure (LIBERO-Goal; bootstrap, 10{,}000 resamples). RSA best-vs-best yields a modest geometric gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$; LIBERO-Spatial: $+0.023$, $p{<}0.001$), while $R^2$ shows substantially larger functional gains.}\n597\t\\label{tab:training_effects}\n598\t\\small\n599\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PgUbf2GtzpaJ7DKZXZBReG","session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"37ff79a9-5cf7-4d34-b6fe-025b636de25a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:27:06.466Z"}
