{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018pWsj3F71Y5fPHh353Y6H9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review:\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Instruction Routing in Fine-Tuned Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three VLA models (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites, using layer-wise action probes, L1 attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbations. The central findings are: (1) VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning produces a consistent \"exit collapse\" in action-probe R^2 (Delta=0.156--0.455) that is absent in VLA-to-VLA transitions and NLP controls; (2) trained VLAs show substantial counterfactual sensitivity to instruction perturbation (Cohen's d=0.45--1.52); and (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention patterns are largely inherited from pretrained backbones rather than created by VLA training. The paper positions itself as a decomposition of training-associated versus inherited instruction routing in VLAs.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Genuine cross-architecture validation is a meaningful contribution.** The 3x4 architecture-suite matrix (10/12 cells filled) across three genuinely different backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL) is the strongest aspect of the paper. Most VLA interpretability work (Haon 2025, Swann 2026, Molinari 2025, Lu 2025) targets a single architecture. The consistent VLM-to-VLA vs. VLA-to-VLA separation in exit collapse (6/6 vs. 0/4 cells exceeding the 0.10 threshold, permutation p=0.008) is a clean empirical result that goes beyond what any single-architecture study could establish.\n\n**S2. The trained/reference decomposition is well-structured.** The distinction between training-associated effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) and inherited effects (L1 attention) is a useful conceptual contribution. The NLP control experiment (Section 4, flat R^2 profiles with Delta=0.000 for all three chat-tuned backbones) is a strong control that rules out exit collapse as a generic fine-tuning artifact.\n\n**S3. Honest and thorough limitation reporting.** The paper is unusually candid about its weaknesses: metric incomparability between Cohen's d and cosine distance (Section 5.1--5.2), the ad hoc Delta>0.10 threshold (with threshold sensitivity analysis in Appendix), RSA/CKA divergence, absence of behavioral validation, and the N=2 VLM-to-VLA sample size. The threshold robustness analysis (any threshold in the interval 0.097--0.156 yields identical grouping) is a commendable piece of due diligence. These should be treated kindly per CoRL norms.\n\n**S4. Careful statistical methodology.** Monte Carlo cross-validation with demo-level resampling (n=1000), label permutation controls, probe seed sensitivity (5 seeds, std < 0.002), and Bonferroni correction across 63 tests reflect thorough experimental practice. The paper explicitly addresses the leakage concern from random frame splitting vs. demo-level splitting and reports the resulting R^2 reduction.\n\n**S5. The related work section is substantially improved from what one might expect.** It covers five relevant concurrent works (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Buurmeijer/Rizzardo 2026, Fang 2026, Lian 2026) and appropriately distinguishes behavioral from representational approaches. The concurrent work paragraph (Section 2, paragraph 3) correctly positions the paper's perturbation analysis as measuring \"how much\" vs. Fang et al.'s \"whether.\"\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The novelty is primarily empirical application of known tools, not methodological or conceptual innovation.** Every analysis method used -- linear/MLP probes (Alain & Bengio 2017), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), counterfactual perturbation, attention head profiling (Clark et al. 2019) -- is standard in the interpretability literature. The paper's contribution is applying this toolkit systematically to VLAs and discovering cross-architecture regularities. This is a valid form of contribution, but it places the burden of novelty entirely on the empirical findings. The exit collapse finding, while cleanly demonstrated, is a descriptive observation without a mechanistic explanation for *why* VLM-to-VLA transitions produce it and VLA-to-VLA transitions do not. Without such an explanation, the finding remains a diagnostic signature rather than a scientific insight.\n\n**W2. The related work omits important lines of work on representation change during fine-tuning.** The fine-tuning effects literature is cited only through Aghajanyan et al. (2021) on intrinsic dimensionality and Kumar et al. (2022) on feature distortion. Missing are:\n- **Representation collapse / neural collapse literature** (Papyan et al. 2020; Jing et al. 2022), which directly relates to exit-layer R^2 decline and could provide theoretical grounding for why exit collapse occurs.\n- **Probing work on how fine-tuning reshapes layer-wise representations in LLMs** (e.g., Merchant et al. 2020 on BERT fine-tuning; Mosbach et al. 2020). The exit collapse is reminiscent of well-known findings that fine-tuning concentrates task-relevant information in later layers while disrupting general representations.\n- **The \"lazy training\" / feature reuse literature** (Chizat et al. 2019), which could explain why VLA-to-VLA transitions show minimal representational change (the model is already in the right basin).\n- **Multimodal representation analysis** beyond VLAs: work on how VLMs process visual vs. textual information internally (e.g., Palit et al. 2023 on vision-language alignment in CLIP's joint space) would contextualize the finding that L1 attention patterns are inherited.\n\nThe related work positions the paper against VLA-specific interpretability work but insufficiently engages with the broader transformer representation analysis literature that could explain the observed phenomena.\n\n**W3. The \"exit collapse\" may be a straightforward consequence of action-head format specialization, not a deep reorganization.** When a VLM is first fine-tuned to produce actions, the exit layer must learn to format representations for the action head's specific decoding scheme (7-DoF tokenized actions). A generic MLP probe trained on intermediate layers measures action-relevant information in a format-agnostic way, but at the exit layer, information may be encoded in a format that the probe cannot access even though the action head can. The paper's own report.md mentions an \"action_head_decoding\" experiment (marked \"negative\") where the frozen action head applied to each layer's hidden states did *not* show the expected monotonic/exit-peaking pattern -- this would have been the key experiment to distinguish format specialization from genuine information loss, but its negative result is not discussed in the paper. This omission is concerning because it means the most parsimonious explanation for exit collapse -- format specialization at the exit layer -- has not been ruled out.\n\n**W4. The cross-architecture \"diversity\" is limited in important ways.** All three architectures are decoder-only causal transformers with frozen (or mostly frozen) vision encoders, trained with autoregressive action token prediction on LIBERO. The paper acknowledges this limitation (Discussion, line 5) but does not sufficiently emphasize that this means the findings may not generalize to:\n- Flow-matching architectures (pi0/pi0.5) which use continuous action heads\n- Diffusion-based policies (Octo, Diffusion Policy)\n- Architectures with trainable vision encoders\n- Encoder-decoder architectures\n\nGiven that Grant et al. (2026) already showed that multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) behave qualitatively differently from single-pathway VLAs, the generality claim should be more carefully scoped. The paper's title (\"Cross-Architecture\") slightly oversells the actual architectural diversity.\n\n**W5. Actionable implications for VLA design are thin.** The paper suggests three practical takeaways: (1) Delta R^2 > 0.10 as a diagnostic screening signal, (2) staged pipelines that preserve exit structure, and (3) uniform pruning over attention-based head selection. However:\n- The correlation between Delta R^2 and published success rates is non-significant (rho=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) -- the paper honestly reports this, but it undermines the diagnostic utility claim.\n- The staged pipeline suggestion is speculative -- no experiment tests whether preserving exit structure actually improves downstream performance.\n- The pruning suggestion derives from an 8% gap in specificity (d=0.65 vs. 0.60), which is small and based on a single architecture.\n\nFor a CoRL submission, which prioritizes robot learning contributions, the lack of any closed-loop behavioral validation is notable. The paper is purely open-loop representational analysis on logged demonstrations.\n\n**W6. The instruction invariance finding (Appendix, Figure 5b) is architecturally trivial but presented without sufficient acknowledgment.** The finding that image_mean representations are invariant to instruction content (max |Delta| < 0.006) is a direct consequence of causal masking: image tokens precede instruction tokens and cannot attend to them. The paper acknowledges this in Section 3.2 (\"the causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions by construction\") but then still presents instruction invariance at image_mean as an empirical finding worth reporting. This inflates the apparent volume of results without adding genuine information.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The action_head_decoding experiment (mentioned in report.md as \"negative\") seems directly relevant to distinguishing format specialization from information loss at the exit layer. Why was this result excluded from the paper? If the frozen action head applied layer-by-layer does *not* show exit-peaking R^2, wouldn't this actually *support* (rather than undermine) the format specialization interpretation?\n\n**Q2.** The N=2 for VLM-to-VLA transitions is a significant limitation. Have you considered testing additional VLM-to-VLA pairs (e.g., fine-tuning Phi-3-Vision or Qwen2.5-VL on a non-LIBERO action dataset) to increase the sample size for the exit collapse claim?\n\n**Q3.** You report that OpenVLA Long-Horizon shows systematically lower counterfactual sensitivity (d=0.45--0.57) compared to other suites (d=0.81--1.52). You attribute this to \"OFT finetuning\" but do not elaborate. Could this be evidence that exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are actually measuring different things -- and that the Long-Horizon tasks, which require multi-step reasoning, engage different routing mechanisms?\n\n**Q4.** The L1 attention finding -- that instruction-dominant heads are inherited rather than trained -- is interesting but raises a question: if L1 routing is entirely inherited, what exactly does VLA training change about instruction processing? The exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are measured at different points (exit layer, action space), so the paper lacks a clear mechanistic story connecting L1 inherited routing to the training-associated effects at later layers.\n\n**Q5.** The permutation test for exit collapse (p=0.008) tests the gap between min(VLM-to-VLA) and max(VLA-to-VLA). But with N=9 values and a 5:4 split, how many unique permutations exist? Is the p-value stable, and have you considered an exact permutation test rather than sampling 10,000 random permutations?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** Table 1: The NORA-Goal Delta R^2 of 0.455 is flagged with footnote \"b\" (bootstrap CI unreliable, exit-layer R^2 approximately -0.006). A negative exit-layer R^2 suggests the probe is worse than a constant predictor at the exit layer. This should be discussed more prominently -- it may indicate that NORA's exit representations are qualitatively degenerate rather than exhibiting a smooth \"collapse.\"\n\n**M2.** The paper uses \"routing\" extensively but never formally defines what routing means in the context of decoder-only transformers with causal masking. In standard transformer circuits terminology, \"routing\" typically refers to how attention heads direct information flow. The paper uses it more loosely to describe how instruction information is processed across layers.\n\n**M3.** Section 5.4 (Synthesis): \"trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks (R^2 = 0.637 at L9 vs. 0.634 at L11, Delta = 0.003)\" -- this numerical similarity could be coincidental given the different scales, architectures, and training data. The claim of \"convergence toward a shared mid-layer structure\" is not supported by a single R^2 value comparison.\n\n**M4.** The title on the main.tex file (\"Instruction Routing in Fine-Tuned Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\") differs from the title mentioned in the task prompt. The current title is more accurate; the \"Token-Level Routing Analysis\" framing in the earlier version was less precise.\n\n**M5.** References: Fang 2025 (cited as DualVLA in the bib) and Fang 2026 (cited as counterfactual VLA) are different author groups but cited with the same last name. This may confuse readers -- consider using first initials in citations.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\nThis is a competent empirical study that applies standard interpretability tools to VLAs and discovers a clean empirical regularity: exit collapse is specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions. The cross-architecture validation is the paper's strongest element, and the experimental methodology is thorough with appropriate controls. However, the paper's novelty is limited to empirical application of existing methods, the findings are descriptive rather than mechanistic (no explanation for *why* exit collapse occurs), and the practical implications remain unvalidated. The related work engages well with concurrent VLA interpretability literature but insufficiently with the broader representation analysis and neural collapse literatures that could explain the observed phenomena. For CoRL, the absence of any behavioral validation (even a simple correlation between exit collapse magnitude and task success on held-out rollouts) is a notable gap. The paper is above average in execution but below the threshold for clear novelty contribution.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Overall Recommendation** | **5** (marginally below acceptance threshold) |\n| **Confidence** | **4** (high -- I am familiar with both the interpretability methods and the VLA literature) |\n| **Novelty** | **2** (limited -- existing methods applied to a new domain; the exit collapse finding is empirically clean but descriptive) |\n| **Clarity** | **3** (good -- well-written, careful qualifications, but \"routing\" terminology is used loosely) |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6a4cb896c7677648 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6a4cb896c7677648' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84215\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 162974</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"ddff4a72-a729-4967-b5e4-3c490aee584c","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:29:37.938Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (OpenReview, ~32% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above threshold\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nConfidence: 1 (low) to 5 (high)\n\nCoRL requires relevance to robot learning. Papers must include a limitations section; treat honestly reported limitations kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\nThis is an analysis/interpretability paper (not a new model). It studies how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing at the token level. It examines 3 architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**Core claims:**\n1. **Routing reorganization:** VLA training shifts instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U to entry-exit \"bookend\" pattern. 24/32 heads instruction-dominant at L1 (vs 9/32 pretrained).\n2. **R² exit collapse:** One-time reorganization at VLM→VLA boundary (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455), absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells).\n3. **Three-tier routing gradient:** VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n4. **Vision encoder geometry (qualified observation):** Non-monotonic RSA profile, but CKA reverses the ordering. Explicitly acknowledged as metric-dependent.\n\n**Paper structure:**\n- §1 Intro: frames the problem as \"how does VLA training reshape instruction routing\"\n- §2 Related Work: 3 short paragraphs (VLA models, representation analysis in transformers, instruction following in LMs)\n- §3 Experimental Framework: models/data, 4 analysis methods (cross-instruction probe, attention profiling, R² action probe, counterfactual perturbation), RSA/CKA, factorial design\n- §4 Token-Level Specialization: frozen encoder dominance, DINOv2 baseline, instruction invariance of image tokens (3 controls)\n- §5 Instruction Routing Reorganization: three routing strategies, L1 attention reorganization, R² exit collapse, cross-architecture validation, counterfactual sensitivity, per-layer profiles\n- §6 Cross-Benchmark Replication\n- §7 Vision Encoder Geometry: RSA vs CKA divergence, cross-encoder comparison, fused backbone decomposition\n- §8 Discussion: what VLA training does, three-tier gradient, limitations (5 items)\n- §9 Conclusion\n\n**Key limitations acknowledged:**\n1. Metric incomparability (Cohen's d vs cosine distance across conditions)\n2. RSA/CKA metric dependence\n3. No behavioral validation\n4. Ad hoc Δ>0.10 threshold\n5. All decoder-only with frozen vision encoders\n\n**Related work landscape you should consider:**\n- Representation analysis in transformers: probing classifiers (Belinkov & Glass 2019), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019)\n- VLA models: RT-2 (Brohan et al. 2023), OpenVLA (Kim et al. 2024), TracVLA, NORA\n- Instruction following in LLMs: instruction tuning analysis (Wei et al., Wang et al.)\n- Behavioral VLA analysis: studies showing visual dominance and instruction underutilization\n- Mechanistic interpretability: circuit analysis, causal tracing, activation patching\n- Information flow in transformers: residual stream analysis, attention knockout\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning focus)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work focus)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n7. **Scores:** Overall recommendation (1-10), Confidence (1-5), Novelty (1-4), Clarity (1-4)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay particular attention to:\n- Is this genuinely novel or incremental application of known interpretability tools to VLAs?\n- How does this compare to existing mechanistic interpretability work on multimodal models?\n- Is the related work section adequate? What's missing?\n- Does the paper offer actionable insights for VLA design, or is it purely descriptive?\n- Is the \"routing reorganization\" framing genuinely new vs. obvious from architecture?\n- How does the instruction invariance finding (§4.3) relate to prior work on causal masking?\n- Is the cross-architecture validation truly diverse enough?\n- Does the paper's scope match CoRL's focus on robot learning?\n- Is the claim about \"diagnostic signatures\" substantiated?\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Do NOT be generic — cite specific sections, claims, and numbers.","agentId":"a6a4cb896c7677648","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review:\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Instruction Routing in Fine-Tuned Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three VLA models (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites, using layer-wise action probes, L1 attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbations. The central findings are: (1) VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning produces a consistent \"exit collapse\" in action-probe R^2 (Delta=0.156--0.455) that is absent in VLA-to-VLA transitions and NLP controls; (2) trained VLAs show substantial counterfactual sensitivity to instruction perturbation (Cohen's d=0.45--1.52); and (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention patterns are largely inherited from pretrained backbones rather than created by VLA training. The paper positions itself as a decomposition of training-associated versus inherited instruction routing in VLAs.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Genuine cross-architecture validation is a meaningful contribution.** The 3x4 architecture-suite matrix (10/12 cells filled) across three genuinely different backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL) is the strongest aspect of the paper. Most VLA interpretability work (Haon 2025, Swann 2026, Molinari 2025, Lu 2025) targets a single architecture. The consistent VLM-to-VLA vs. VLA-to-VLA separation in exit collapse (6/6 vs. 0/4 cells exceeding the 0.10 threshold, permutation p=0.008) is a clean empirical result that goes beyond what any single-architecture study could establish.\n\n**S2. The trained/reference decomposition is well-structured.** The distinction between training-associated effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) and inherited effects (L1 attention) is a useful conceptual contribution. The NLP control experiment (Section 4, flat R^2 profiles with Delta=0.000 for all three chat-tuned backbones) is a strong control that rules out exit collapse as a generic fine-tuning artifact.\n\n**S3. Honest and thorough limitation reporting.** The paper is unusually candid about its weaknesses: metric incomparability between Cohen's d and cosine distance (Section 5.1--5.2), the ad hoc Delta>0.10 threshold (with threshold sensitivity analysis in Appendix), RSA/CKA divergence, absence of behavioral validation, and the N=2 VLM-to-VLA sample size. The threshold robustness analysis (any threshold in the interval 0.097--0.156 yields identical grouping) is a commendable piece of due diligence. These should be treated kindly per CoRL norms.\n\n**S4. Careful statistical methodology.** Monte Carlo cross-validation with demo-level resampling (n=1000), label permutation controls, probe seed sensitivity (5 seeds, std < 0.002), and Bonferroni correction across 63 tests reflect thorough experimental practice. The paper explicitly addresses the leakage concern from random frame splitting vs. demo-level splitting and reports the resulting R^2 reduction.\n\n**S5. The related work section is substantially improved from what one might expect.** It covers five relevant concurrent works (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Buurmeijer/Rizzardo 2026, Fang 2026, Lian 2026) and appropriately distinguishes behavioral from representational approaches. The concurrent work paragraph (Section 2, paragraph 3) correctly positions the paper's perturbation analysis as measuring \"how much\" vs. Fang et al.'s \"whether.\"\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The novelty is primarily empirical application of known tools, not methodological or conceptual innovation.** Every analysis method used -- linear/MLP probes (Alain & Bengio 2017), RSA (Kriegeskorte 2008), CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), counterfactual perturbation, attention head profiling (Clark et al. 2019) -- is standard in the interpretability literature. The paper's contribution is applying this toolkit systematically to VLAs and discovering cross-architecture regularities. This is a valid form of contribution, but it places the burden of novelty entirely on the empirical findings. The exit collapse finding, while cleanly demonstrated, is a descriptive observation without a mechanistic explanation for *why* VLM-to-VLA transitions produce it and VLA-to-VLA transitions do not. Without such an explanation, the finding remains a diagnostic signature rather than a scientific insight.\n\n**W2. The related work omits important lines of work on representation change during fine-tuning.** The fine-tuning effects literature is cited only through Aghajanyan et al. (2021) on intrinsic dimensionality and Kumar et al. (2022) on feature distortion. Missing are:\n- **Representation collapse / neural collapse literature** (Papyan et al. 2020; Jing et al. 2022), which directly relates to exit-layer R^2 decline and could provide theoretical grounding for why exit collapse occurs.\n- **Probing work on how fine-tuning reshapes layer-wise representations in LLMs** (e.g., Merchant et al. 2020 on BERT fine-tuning; Mosbach et al. 2020). The exit collapse is reminiscent of well-known findings that fine-tuning concentrates task-relevant information in later layers while disrupting general representations.\n- **The \"lazy training\" / feature reuse literature** (Chizat et al. 2019), which could explain why VLA-to-VLA transitions show minimal representational change (the model is already in the right basin).\n- **Multimodal representation analysis** beyond VLAs: work on how VLMs process visual vs. textual information internally (e.g., Palit et al. 2023 on vision-language alignment in CLIP's joint space) would contextualize the finding that L1 attention patterns are inherited.\n\nThe related work positions the paper against VLA-specific interpretability work but insufficiently engages with the broader transformer representation analysis literature that could explain the observed phenomena.\n\n**W3. The \"exit collapse\" may be a straightforward consequence of action-head format specialization, not a deep reorganization.** When a VLM is first fine-tuned to produce actions, the exit layer must learn to format representations for the action head's specific decoding scheme (7-DoF tokenized actions). A generic MLP probe trained on intermediate layers measures action-relevant information in a format-agnostic way, but at the exit layer, information may be encoded in a format that the probe cannot access even though the action head can. The paper's own report.md mentions an \"action_head_decoding\" experiment (marked \"negative\") where the frozen action head applied to each layer's hidden states did *not* show the expected monotonic/exit-peaking pattern -- this would have been the key experiment to distinguish format specialization from genuine information loss, but its negative result is not discussed in the paper. This omission is concerning because it means the most parsimonious explanation for exit collapse -- format specialization at the exit layer -- has not been ruled out.\n\n**W4. The cross-architecture \"diversity\" is limited in important ways.** All three architectures are decoder-only causal transformers with frozen (or mostly frozen) vision encoders, trained with autoregressive action token prediction on LIBERO. The paper acknowledges this limitation (Discussion, line 5) but does not sufficiently emphasize that this means the findings may not generalize to:\n- Flow-matching architectures (pi0/pi0.5) which use continuous action heads\n- Diffusion-based policies (Octo, Diffusion Policy)\n- Architectures with trainable vision encoders\n- Encoder-decoder architectures\n\nGiven that Grant et al. (2026) already showed that multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) behave qualitatively differently from single-pathway VLAs, the generality claim should be more carefully scoped. The paper's title (\"Cross-Architecture\") slightly oversells the actual architectural diversity.\n\n**W5. Actionable implications for VLA design are thin.** The paper suggests three practical takeaways: (1) Delta R^2 > 0.10 as a diagnostic screening signal, (2) staged pipelines that preserve exit structure, and (3) uniform pruning over attention-based head selection. However:\n- The correlation between Delta R^2 and published success rates is non-significant (rho=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) -- the paper honestly reports this, but it undermines the diagnostic utility claim.\n- The staged pipeline suggestion is speculative -- no experiment tests whether preserving exit structure actually improves downstream performance.\n- The pruning suggestion derives from an 8% gap in specificity (d=0.65 vs. 0.60), which is small and based on a single architecture.\n\nFor a CoRL submission, which prioritizes robot learning contributions, the lack of any closed-loop behavioral validation is notable. The paper is purely open-loop representational analysis on logged demonstrations.\n\n**W6. The instruction invariance finding (Appendix, Figure 5b) is architecturally trivial but presented without sufficient acknowledgment.** The finding that image_mean representations are invariant to instruction content (max |Delta| < 0.006) is a direct consequence of causal masking: image tokens precede instruction tokens and cannot attend to them. The paper acknowledges this in Section 3.2 (\"the causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions by construction\") but then still presents instruction invariance at image_mean as an empirical finding worth reporting. This inflates the apparent volume of results without adding genuine information.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The action_head_decoding experiment (mentioned in report.md as \"negative\") seems directly relevant to distinguishing format specialization from information loss at the exit layer. Why was this result excluded from the paper? If the frozen action head applied layer-by-layer does *not* show exit-peaking R^2, wouldn't this actually *support* (rather than undermine) the format specialization interpretation?\n\n**Q2.** The N=2 for VLM-to-VLA transitions is a significant limitation. Have you considered testing additional VLM-to-VLA pairs (e.g., fine-tuning Phi-3-Vision or Qwen2.5-VL on a non-LIBERO action dataset) to increase the sample size for the exit collapse claim?\n\n**Q3.** You report that OpenVLA Long-Horizon shows systematically lower counterfactual sensitivity (d=0.45--0.57) compared to other suites (d=0.81--1.52). You attribute this to \"OFT finetuning\" but do not elaborate. Could this be evidence that exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are actually measuring different things -- and that the Long-Horizon tasks, which require multi-step reasoning, engage different routing mechanisms?\n\n**Q4.** The L1 attention finding -- that instruction-dominant heads are inherited rather than trained -- is interesting but raises a question: if L1 routing is entirely inherited, what exactly does VLA training change about instruction processing? The exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are measured at different points (exit layer, action space), so the paper lacks a clear mechanistic story connecting L1 inherited routing to the training-associated effects at later layers.\n\n**Q5.** The permutation test for exit collapse (p=0.008) tests the gap between min(VLM-to-VLA) and max(VLA-to-VLA). But with N=9 values and a 5:4 split, how many unique permutations exist? Is the p-value stable, and have you considered an exact permutation test rather than sampling 10,000 random permutations?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** Table 1: The NORA-Goal Delta R^2 of 0.455 is flagged with footnote \"b\" (bootstrap CI unreliable, exit-layer R^2 approximately -0.006). A negative exit-layer R^2 suggests the probe is worse than a constant predictor at the exit layer. This should be discussed more prominently -- it may indicate that NORA's exit representations are qualitatively degenerate rather than exhibiting a smooth \"collapse.\"\n\n**M2.** The paper uses \"routing\" extensively but never formally defines what routing means in the context of decoder-only transformers with causal masking. In standard transformer circuits terminology, \"routing\" typically refers to how attention heads direct information flow. The paper uses it more loosely to describe how instruction information is processed across layers.\n\n**M3.** Section 5.4 (Synthesis): \"trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks (R^2 = 0.637 at L9 vs. 0.634 at L11, Delta = 0.003)\" -- this numerical similarity could be coincidental given the different scales, architectures, and training data. The claim of \"convergence toward a shared mid-layer structure\" is not supported by a single R^2 value comparison.\n\n**M4.** The title on the main.tex file (\"Instruction Routing in Fine-Tuned Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\") differs from the title mentioned in the task prompt. The current title is more accurate; the \"Token-Level Routing Analysis\" framing in the earlier version was less precise.\n\n**M5.** References: Fang 2025 (cited as DualVLA in the bib) and Fang 2026 (cited as counterfactual VLA) are different author groups but cited with the same last name. This may confuse readers -- consider using first initials in citations.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\nThis is a competent empirical study that applies standard interpretability tools to VLAs and discovers a clean empirical regularity: exit collapse is specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions. The cross-architecture validation is the paper's strongest element, and the experimental methodology is thorough with appropriate controls. However, the paper's novelty is limited to empirical application of existing methods, the findings are descriptive rather than mechanistic (no explanation for *why* exit collapse occurs), and the practical implications remain unvalidated. The related work engages well with concurrent VLA interpretability literature but insufficiently with the broader representation analysis and neural collapse literatures that could explain the observed phenomena. For CoRL, the absence of any behavioral validation (even a simple correlation between exit collapse magnitude and task success on held-out rollouts) is a notable gap. The paper is above average in execution but below the threshold for clear novelty contribution.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Overall Recommendation** | **5** (marginally below acceptance threshold) |\n| **Confidence** | **4** (high -- I am familiar with both the interpretability methods and the VLA literature) |\n| **Novelty** | **2** (limited -- existing methods applied to a new domain; the exit collapse finding is empirically clean but descriptive) |\n| **Clarity** | **3** (good -- well-written, careful qualifications, but \"routing\" terminology is used loosely) |"}],"totalDurationMs":162974,"totalTokens":84215,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":730,"cache_read_input_tokens":79812,"output_tokens":3670,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":730},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3670,"cache_read_input_tokens":79812,"cache_creation_input_tokens":730,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":730,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QzgRfiY5AETcThSGrUSsMQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviews are complete. Now I need to conduct the roundtable process:\n\n**Step 1: Independent reviews** — Done (R1 and R2 above)\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing** — R1 reads R2's review and vice versa, each comments on what they agree/disagree with\n**Step 3: Convergence** — Identify areas of agreement and remaining disagreements\n**Step 4: Synthesis** — Produce the final consolidated review report\n\nLet me now do Steps 2-3 by analyzing both reviews and identifying convergence/divergence, then produce the Step 4 consolidated report.\n\n\nNow I'm extracting the core findings from each review. R1 gave a 5 for methods and experiments, highlighting the rigorous cross-architecture design and statistical approaches, though flagging concerns about the small sample of VLM-to-VLA transitions, the arbitrary threshold choice, and metric inconsistencies. R2 also scored 5 for novelty and related work coverage, praising the genuine cross-modal contribution, though the summary cuts off mid-thought. R2 identifies a critical distinction about format specialization versus information loss that R1 misses, and raises important gaps around neural collapse literature and the architectural triviality of instruction invariance. R1 separately flags the problematic inclusion of NORA Goal's negative R² in the headline range. Both converge on a score of 5, which maps to a weak reject on the CoRL scale, so I'll consolidate around that consensus while documenting where their critiques diverge.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11090,"cache_read_input_tokens":65283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11090},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnosti