{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QzgRfiY5AETcThSGrUSsMQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011hFhbb5bwBWJY11sM3EFhL","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — CoRL 2026 Submission\n\n**Paper:** \"Token-Level Routing Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models: Cross-Architecture Evidence for Instruction Routing Reorganization\"\n\n**Venue:** CoRL 2026 (acceptance rate ~32%, OpenReview scale 1–10)\n\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## Venue Calibration\n\nCoRL uses an OpenReview 1–10 scale. Submissions are evaluated on significance and novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), relevance to robot learning, and must include a limitations section. Reviewers are instructed to treat honestly reported limitations kindly. Acceptance rate ~32%.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 — Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nCross-architecture representational analysis of three VLA models (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) across four LIBERO suites, studying how VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction routing. Using layer-wise R² probes, L1 attention profiling, and counterfactual perturbation, the authors identify exit collapse as specific to VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells) and absent in VLA→VLA transitions (0/4 cells) and NLP controls.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1. Well-designed cross-architecture matrix with appropriate controls.** 3×4 architecture-suite matrix (10/12 cells) with NLP finetuning controls (Llama-2-7b-chat, Phi-3-mini-instruct, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct) sharing backbones.\n- **S2. Statistical rigor above typical standard.** MCCV with n=1000 demo-level splits, 10,000-iteration bootstrap, permutation tests (p=0.008), TOST equivalence testing, Bonferroni correction (40/45 survive), probe seed sensitivity (5 seeds, std < 0.002).\n- **S3. Honest limitation reporting.** Metric incomparability (Cohen's d vs cosine distance), ad hoc Δ>0.10 threshold, RSA/CKA divergence, and behavioral correlation failure (ρ=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) are all candidly disclosed.\n- **S4. Convergent evidence from distinct methods.** Probing, attention profiling, and counterfactual perturbation address different aspects; their convergence strengthens the overall characterization.\n- **S5. Careful confound checking.** Probe architecture sensitivity (linear vs MLP), distance metric invariance (cosine vs Euclidean, r>0.989), demonstration subsample sensitivity, activation diagnostics for pretrained LLMs under visual input.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1. N=2 independent VLM→VLA transitions.** The core claim rests on exactly two independent VLM→VLA comparisons (TracVLA, NORA). The permutation test (p=0.008) provides some statistical reassurance, but structural diversity does not substitute for independent replications. A third counterexample would break the pattern.\n- **W2. Δ>0.10 threshold lacks theoretical grounding.** The sensitivity analysis shows the clean gap spans [0.097, 0.156], but this is a post-hoc observation. The gap width of 0.059 means a future architecture could easily fall in the ambiguous range. A continuous measure (actual Δ R² plotted against transition type) would be more informative than a binary threshold.\n- **W3. Metric incomparability is unresolved.** For 6/10 counterfactual cells, trained/reference comparison uses different metrics (Cohen's d vs cosine distance). Only within OpenVLA is same-metric comparison possible, and there the effect is modest (Goal: Δ=+0.04; Spatial: Δ=+0.24). The counterfactual sensitivity contribution is largely descriptive for TracVLA and NORA.\n- **W4. No closed-loop behavioral validation.** The paper reports a failed attempt at closed-loop evaluation (SR=0/3). For CoRL, the disconnect between representational findings and robot behavior is a notable gap. The practical claims (Δ R² as screening signal, mid-layer features outperforming exit, uniform pruning) have no empirical validation.\n- **W5. NLP control is informative but limited.** Flat R²≈0.155 across all layers in NLP controls is the noise floor (these models have never seen robot actions). This tells us NLP finetuning does not *create* action structure, not that it fails to *reshape* existing structure the way VLA training does.\n- **W6. NORA Goal data point is unreliable.** Exit-layer R²≈-0.006 may indicate probe failure rather than genuine information loss, yet Δ=0.455 is included in the headline range (0.156–0.455). The reduced range (0.156–0.362) excluding NORA Goal is more defensible.\n\n### Key Questions\n1. Could absence of exit collapse in OpenVLA simply mean OXE pretraining already underwent collapse? Plotting absolute exit-layer R² values (not just deltas) would clarify.\n2. The 8% gap in matched-size ablation (d=0.65 vs 0.60) suggests routing is quite distributed. Does this undermine the emphasis on instruction-dominant heads as a meaningful category?\n3. How to distinguish the \"three-tier gradient\" from a simpler VLM→VLA/VLA→VLA binary, given Tier 2 has N=1 architecture?\n\n### Scores\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Overall | **5** (marginally below threshold) |\n| Confidence | **4** (high) |\n| Soundness | **3/4** (good, but N=2 and no behavioral validation) |\n| Significance | **2/4** (descriptively interesting, limited actionable impact) |\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nSame paper summary as R1. The paper applies standard interpretability tools (probing, RSA, CKA, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation) systematically to VLAs and discovers cross-architecture regularities, primarily exit collapse at the VLM→VLA boundary.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1. Genuine cross-architecture validation.** 3×4 matrix across genuinely different backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5-VL). Most VLA interpretability work targets single architectures.\n- **S2. Clean trained/reference decomposition.** Distinguishing training-associated effects (exit collapse, CF sensitivity) from inherited effects (L1 attention) is a useful conceptual contribution.\n- **S3. Unusually candid limitations.** Five explicitly stated limitations with supporting analyses (threshold sensitivity, behavioral correlation p-value).\n- **S4. Engagement with concurrent work.** Five concurrent VLA interpretability papers are discussed and differentiated appropriately.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1. Novelty is empirical application, not methodological or conceptual innovation.** Every method (probes, RSA, CKA, counterfactual perturbation, attention profiling) is standard in interpretability literature. The contribution is applying this toolkit to VLAs and discovering regularities. This places the burden entirely on empirical findings, and the exit collapse remains descriptive without a mechanistic explanation for *why* VLM→VLA produces it.\n- **W2. Related work omits important literatures:**\n  - **Neural collapse / representation collapse** (Papyan et al. 2020, Jing et al. 2022) — directly relates to exit-layer R² decline and could provide theoretical grounding.\n  - **Fine-tuning effects on layer-wise representations** (Merchant et al. 2020 on BERT, Mosbach et al. 2020) — exit collapse is reminiscent of known findings that fine-tuning concentrates task info in later layers while disrupting general representations.\n  - **Lazy training / feature reuse** (Chizat et al. 2019) — could explain why VLA→VLA shows minimal change.\n  - **Multimodal representation analysis beyond VLAs** (e.g., Palit et al. 2023 on CLIP).\n- **W3. Exit collapse may be format specialization, not deep reorganization.** When a VLM first learns to produce actions, the exit layer must format representations for the action head's tokenization scheme. An MLP probe on intermediate layers is format-agnostic, but at the exit layer, information may be encoded in an action-head-specific format the probe cannot decode. An \"action_head_decoding\" experiment (applying the frozen action head layer-by-layer) would distinguish format specialization from genuine information loss, but the review context suggests this experiment gave negative results and was excluded from the paper. This is the most parsimonious alternative explanation and it has not been ruled out.\n- **W4. \"Cross-architecture\" diversity is narrower than title suggests.** All three architectures are decoder-only causal transformers with frozen vision encoders trained on LIBERO with autoregressive action token prediction. Flow-matching (pi0/pi0.5), diffusion-based (Octo), trainable-encoder, and encoder-decoder architectures are not covered.\n- **W5. Actionable implications are thin and unvalidated.**\n  - Δ R² as diagnostic: behavioral correlation is non-significant (ρ=0.583, p=0.10, n=9).\n  - Staged pipelines preserving exit structure: no experiment tests whether this improves downstream performance.\n  - Uniform pruning recommendation: derives from an 8% gap in a single architecture.\n- **W6. Instruction invariance (§4.3) is architecturally trivial.** Image tokens precede instruction tokens under causal masking and cannot attend to them. Reporting this as an empirical finding inflates the apparent volume of results.\n\n### Key Questions\n1. Why was the action_head_decoding experiment excluded? If the frozen action head applied layer-by-layer doesn't show exit-peaking R², this supports format specialization as the explanation for exit collapse.\n2. Have you considered additional VLM→VLA pairs beyond LIBERO to increase N for the core claim?\n3. OpenVLA Long-Horizon shows systematically lower CF sensitivity (d=0.45–0.57 vs 0.81–1.52). Could this indicate exit collapse and CF sensitivity measure different constructs?\n4. If L1 routing is inherited, what exactly does VLA training change about instruction processing? The paper lacks a clear mechanistic story connecting L1 to later-layer effects.\n\n### Scores\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Overall | **5** (marginally below threshold) |\n| Confidence | **4** (high) |\n| Novelty | **2/4** (existing methods applied to new domain; exit collapse is clean but descriptive) |\n| Clarity | **3/4** (well-written, careful qualifications, \"routing\" used loosely) |\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Synthesis (Steps 2–4)\n\n### Areas of Full Agreement\n1. **Score:** Both reviewers independently assign 5 (marginally below acceptance threshold).\n2. **Core strength:** The cross-architecture matrix with NLP controls is well-designed and the statistical methodology is above typical standards for interpretability work.\n3. **Core weakness #1:** N=2 independent VLM→VLA transitions is the minimum viable count; a single counterexample would break the generalization.\n4. **Core weakness #2:** No behavioral validation at a venue that centers robot learning. The practical implications (diagnostic signatures, pruning guidance, staged pipelines) are speculative.\n5. **Core weakness #3:** Metric incomparability weakens the counterfactual sensitivity contribution across architectures.\n6. **Positive recognition:** Honest limitation reporting is commendable and treated favorably per CoRL norms.\n\n### Areas of Complementary Critique\n- **R2's format specialization critique (W3)** is the strongest individual concern not raised by R1. If exit collapse reflects the exit layer adopting an action-head-specific format (rather than discarding information), the \"information loss\" interpretation is undermined. The reported-but-excluded action_head_decoding experiment would address this directly.\n- **R2's missing related work (W2)** on neural collapse and fine-tuning effects is a substantive gap that could have strengthened the paper's theoretical grounding.\n- **R1's NORA Goal data quality concern (W6)** is a valid point: negative R² at the exit layer may indicate probe pathology rather than genuine collapse, inflating the reported effect range.\n\n### Remaining Disagreement\nNone on scores. Minor calibration difference: R1 weights the statistical rigor more positively; R2 weights the novelty deficit more negatively. These balance to the same overall score.\n\n### Accept/Reject Recommendation\n\n**Consensus: Weak Reject (5/10)**\n\n**Rationale:** This is a competently executed empirical study that discovers a clean regularity (exit collapse at VLM→VLA boundary) through careful cross-architecture experiments. The statistical methodology is strong and the limitation reporting is exemplary. However:\n\n1. **Novelty is limited to empirical application** of existing interpretability tools; no new methods or mechanistic explanations.\n2. **The core finding (N=2 VLM→VLA)** is suggestive but insufficiently replicated for a strong generalization claim.\n3. **The alternative explanation (format specialization)** has not been ruled out, weakening the \"information loss\" interpretation.\n4. **No behavioral validation** at a robotics venue where closed-loop demonstrations are expected. The practical implications are entirely unvalidated.\n5. **Metric incomparability** limits the counterfactual sensitivity contribution to a single architecture (OpenVLA).\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (a) at least one additional VLM→VLA architecture, (b) the action_head_decoding experiment to distinguish format specialization from information loss, (c) even minimal behavioral correlation (e.g., probing-predicted vs actual task success on held-out rollouts), and (d) engagement with the neural collapse literature.\n\n**Estimated rebuttal success probability: ~35%.** The authors could address the threshold concern and format specialization question in text, but adding architectures or behavioral experiments requires new compute. The metric incomparability and N=2 limitations are structural.\n\n### Score Summary Table\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n| Overall | 5 | 5 | **5** (weak reject) |\n| Confidence | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n| Soundness | 3/4 | — | 3/4 |\n| Significance | 2/4 | — | 2/4 |\n| Novelty | — | 2/4 | 2/4 |\n| Clarity | — | 3/4 | 3/4 |\n\n---\n\n## Coverage Assessment vs. Experiment Design\n\nComparing the paper against the review_context.yaml experiment list:\n\n| Experiment | In Paper? | Notes |\n|------------|-----------|-------|\n| plan_001: SigLIP+Projector RSA baseline | ✅ Table 1 | Clean result, uncontested |\n| plan_002: DINO standalone baseline | ✅ Table 3 | Clean result |\n| plan_003: Per-layer RSA curve | ✅ Table 12 | Metric-dependent (CKA reversal) — appropriately qualified |\n| plan_004: Frozen encoder verification | ✅ Table 2 | Clean result |\n| plan_005: 6 probing methods (orphaned) | ❌ Orphaned | Superseded by plan_011, appropriate |\n| plan_006: Instruction ablation RSA | ✅ Table 4 | Clean negative result |\n| plan_007: 2×2 factorial design | ✅ Table 5 | Null result honestly reported |\n| plan_008: Random instruction control | ✅ Table 6 | Thorough controls |\n| plan_009: CKA verification | ✅ Table 14 | Key problematic result — honestly reported RSA/CKA reversal |\n| plan_010: LIBERO-Spatial replication | ✅ Table 13 | Within-arch replication |\n| plan_011: Full-layer R² scan | ✅ Tables 9-10 | Core evidence for exit collapse |\n| plan_012: CLIP-ViT control | ✅ Table 15 | Clean result |\n| plan_013: Branch-split RSA | ✅ Table 16 | Supporting detail |\n\n**Coverage: 12/13 experiments included (1 appropriately orphaned).** The paper faithfully covers the experimental design. The main concern is not missing experiments but rather the interpretive claims made from the results (particularly exit collapse interpretation and metric-dependent geometry claims).\n","project":"vla-ssd","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260527T022433","overall_score":"5 — weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11090,"cache_read_input_tokens":65283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11090},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc4ce46a-8c3b-459f-bd12-119e3d710a0a","uuid":"40625663-ef1e-4589-aded-9cd482f3032f"}
